
module gunbc.design_document

import std.markdown {
  MarkdownDocument, MarkdownBlock,
  HeadingBlock, TextInline, H1, CodeBlock,
}
import gunbc.plans.md_helpers { h2, h3, p, li, li_blocks, ul, ol, hr }
import extdeps.languages.markdown { serialize_markdown_source }
import gunbc.documentary_refs {
  body_lowering_symbol_index_ref,
  body_lowering_lexical_lookup_ref,
  body_lowering_production_consumer_ref,
  documentary_ref_symbol_text,
  five_minute_ci_gate_authority_ref,
  v1_maintenance_standing_ref,
}

fn h1(text: String) -> MarkdownBlock { HeadingBlock { level: H1, inlines: [TextInline { text: text }] } }

fn preamble_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h1(text: "gunbc — Design"),
    p(text: "`README.md` and `CLAUDE.md` symlink here — this is the single source of truth. (v1 ships the `gunbc` CLI and is v2's seed · v3 was removed, migrated into v1 · v2 is active.)"),
    p(text: "This document is reasoned **serially**: §1 fixes the axioms, and each later section is a consequence of the ones before it (or an independent peer) — never a restatement of it. The principles below apply recursively, including to this document. It is a living draft rebuilt from first principles (the prior ~30k-line doc corpus was bankrupted 2026-06-16; it remains in git history). Examples are verified against the live tree or a git-history receipt; open threads are collected at the end."),
    hr(),
  ]
}

fn section_1_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "1. The objective (the axioms)"),
    p(text: "The assumptions everything else rests on. Three **axioms** — assumed, not derived:"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**A1 — there is a goal.** To *solve a problem* presupposes an agent with a goal to satisfy; with no goal there is nothing to optimize."),
      li(text: "**A2 — time is the value.** Every agent intrinsically values time — it is finite and is the substance of acting at all: **time is life**. It is the one value we may assume is *shared*."),
      li(text: "**A3 — agreement is temporal.** Intersubjective agreement — two parties holding the same fact — is possible only *across* time, and only on what stays stable under it."),
    ]),
    p(text: "Three things follow, in order:"),
    ul(items: [
      li_blocks(blocks: [
        p(text: "**From A1 and A2 — the solution is minimal, safe, efficient.** A solution is good exactly insofar as it spends less of the only thing valued, time; and time is spent three ways:"),
        ul(items: [
          li(text: "**cost** — time to run (cheap to run and to feed);"),
          li(text: "**safety** — time to recover from a silent wrong answer, paid later at interest (harm reduction);"),
          li(text: "**complexity** — time to change (it stays cheap to maintain)."),
        ]),
        p(text: "So the three are not preferences layered on — they are A2 applied to A1, and the project optimizes them jointly."),
      ]),
      li(text: "**From A2 and A3 — grounding is intersubjective.** Because time is the *only* assumed-shared value, a fact is grounded only by pointing at a shared, time-stable framework (§4), never an internal taxonomy."),
      li(text: "**At the limit — reduce intersubjectivity to physics.** The deepest such framework — the efficient, time-bound description of interactions that satisfies a goal — is **physics** (A1–A2 turned on grounding itself). So the aim, at its limit, is to *reduce intersubjectivity to physics*: replace convention with necessity until nothing arbitrary survives. This is the deep reason §3 models the universal frameworks and real upstream rather than re-coining them — a nickname is convention standing where physics was available."),
    ]),
    p(text: "The safety axis carries its own measure, used throughout: **safety is measured by how far each error class has climbed from runtime harm toward structural impossibility** — the guarantee ladder; §4b defines the ladder, its ceilings, and its evidence rules."),
  ]
}

fn section_2_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "2. Minimize redundancy (the master move for cost and complexity)"),
    p(text: "Redundant work — **duplicated, unnecessary, or irrelevant** — loses on all three of §1's quantities at once: it costs more to run, it widens the surface where harm hides, and it adds complexity to maintain. So a perfectly DRY process is, *by the meaning of redundant*, the minimal/efficient one; minimizing redundancy is therefore the master move for §1's cost and complexity axes (the safety axis is §5). Through §1's time lens this is *why* DRY matters at all: redundant work **defers** cost into the future — it shoves a problem onto a later fixer, or builds a process destined to be thrown out — so DRY is the refusal to spend someone's future time to buy the author's present convenience; it values time **holistically**, across every party and all of it, not just the here-and-now. Redundancy is removed along exactly two directions — not separate \"DRY rules,\" but one move seen two ways:"),
    ul(items: [
      li_blocks(blocks: [
        p(text: "**Horizontal — one concept, every scale and breadth.** Model a concept once; derive every use (model-local / derive-global). At the right layer there is nothing fundamentally different between scales, so the *same* concept spans nanosecond memoization and broad infra deployment — the Realization pattern (content-addressed pure-spec → host-effect; one kernel, N handlers)."),
        ul(items: [
          li(text: "*e.g.* `dag/std/integer.dag`: `Int8`…`UInt128` are 10 `Compose<Int, MachineWidth<N>>` rows, one axis not 10 types. Realization spans resolve-cost (ns) → sccache → §10 OS provisioning on one content-hash. Cost of *not* doing it: eleven hand-rolled v1 `HashMap` caches, and v2 still recurs (`ParseTable`)."),
        ]),
      ]),
      li_blocks(blocks: [
        p(text: "**Deep — every concept decomposed to grounded atoms.** Nothing is opaque that isn't *genuinely* atomic. The move is `decompress → map → reduce`: reveal the structure the source names, **map** each part onto the concept that already exists (DFS the concept DAG first), **reduce** duplicates. A `String` leaf hiding named parts is anemic modeling."),
        ul(items: [
          li(text: "*e.g.* `\"LGA4926\"` → `CpuSocket \{ package: LandGridArray, contact_count: 4926 \}` (the number is a grounded `Int`) and `\"HMA82GR7AFR4N-VK\"` → `DramModuleCatalogRow \{ chip_width: bit_width(4), rank_count: 1, buffering: Registered \}` (the numeric organization axes decoded from the part number ground into existing magnitudes — `chip_width` reuses `BitWidth`, `rank_count` is a cardinal `Int` — not a fresh enum re-inventing bit-width; the categorical axis stays a closed enum); `Cost = Time|Space|Energy` → a record (every cost has all three); and `\"WDS200T2X0E\"` → `NvmeDeviceCatalogRow \{ pcie_link: PcieLink \{ generation: Gen4, lane_count: 4 \}, form_factor: M2_2280, .. \}` (storage's rank/width: `lane_count` a cardinal `Int`, `PcieGeneration` a closed enum, per-lane bandwidth *derived* not stored — cited to the PCI Express authority in `extdeps/storage/types.dag` and surfaced through `product.compute_fabric`'s `StorageDevice` coproduct on its `NvmeDevice.catalog` variant, parallel to the DRAM row above). (`GpuFacts.vendor`, once a bare `NonEmptyStr` where `CpuVendor` was a closed enum, is now grounded on the single `Vendor<Hardware>` authority — see §3.)"),
        ]),
      ]),
    ]),
    p(text: "The test that an edit actually *reduced* redundancy rather than moving it: **net concepts must not grow by re-invention.** Decomposing a leaf by minting a fresh authority for a concept that already exists is a failed decomposition."),
  ]
}

fn section_3_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "3. Single authority (what keeps §2 from being undone)"),
    p(text: "Minimization holds only if each fact lives in exactly one place. The recurring violation is **nicknaming — a second name for one concept** — which duplicates work at the meaning layer and, since we generate from concepts, duplicates it again in everything derived (testgen, emit, lenses). A fork always gets consolidated later, so it is a correctness concern, not a style one. We cannot enforce this programmatically yet; until then it is diligence — faithfully model the accepted universal frameworks (classical logic, set theory, algebra), and in `extdeps/` the real upstream spec (cite the source, keep its real names, declare its version, model what the API actually returns), rather than re-coining them. Corollaries: the import graph's only structural law is acyclicity — the `std`/`extdeps`/`compiler`/`workflow` folders are browsing conventions, not a direction rule (the former strict layer DAG `std ← extdeps ← compiler ← workflow` was deleted 2026-07-24: a folder-prefix direction was convention standing where no necessity stood, and it blocked correct grounding — a std surface citing the extdeps rows that ground it; the `extdeps/` citation duty, `ExtdepsExternalAuthorityGate`, survives untouched); a fact's home is its *layer*, not its file (paths are discriminators, not gospel); below-boundary representation is opaque (the rename test). A direct consequence for `extdeps/` service operations, three separable facts that must not be fused into one row: (a) the **interface shape** — the dependency's parameterized contract (inputs → outputs, exit/error semantics) — is what extdeps owns; (b) the **transport** (shell / REST / SDK) is a §2 Realization *handler* bound to that shape, *one of N*, not a fact about the dependency — a single hardwired transport is the §4 N×M-adapter trap (`git diff` argv models the git CLI, a nickname for git's diff *semantics*; libgit2 and the compare API are the other handlers); (c) **business policy** (which base ref, which flag, an idempotency protocol the dependency doesn't provide) is a *workflow* fact and modeling it in extdeps is a layer inversion (extdeps depending upward). The tell that (c) has leaked is an argv carrying a literal it should receive as a parameter (`origin/main...HEAD`, `--all-targets`); the tell that (b) has fused is the same operation forked once per transport (GCP's OAuth2 refresh modeled three ways) instead of one shape with N bound handlers. Scaffolded by `v2.lens.extdeps_shape_transport_policy`: lens-unit +/- on synthetic fact rows; corpus RED witnesses execute against live `dag/extdeps/**` via `filesystem_read` (dissolve-on: Node-tree argv projection supersedes text scan). This holds at *any* interface↔realization seam, not only service ops: the cited data behind an agnostic std surface de-fuses exactly as a transport does, and its directional reading is the same — the *dispatch* that selects a realization is itself realization, so it sits peripheral (`extdeps/`), never in the interface (a §2 pure-spec is dispatch-free; a std projection that *matched* over its realizations would have to name them, fusing dispatch back into the pure-spec §2 keeps free of it). What migrates outward is each realization's grounded specifics — cited rows, *or* a per-OS taxonomy reduced to the shape — together with the dispatch; only the agnostic shape stays central. A std type that privileged one realization's taxonomy (POSIX `st_mode` over the `Ntfs`/`Apfs` that `FileSystemSemantics` already enumerates) is itself that inversion."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "*e.g.* `CpuArchitecture` and `TargetArchitecture` are byte-identical enums (the latter's header denies the parallel it declares); `ModulePath` was a nickname for `QualifiedName` (renamed, `ModulePathSegment` deleted); one \"vendor\" concept once forked three ways by rigor — `CpuVendor`/`DramManufacturer` closed enums vs `GpuFacts.vendor` stringly — now dissolved into a single generic `Vendor<Domain>` whose cited company entities live one-file-each in `extdeps/vendor/` (referencing a company *grounds* it as a real upstream entity with its legal name, not a minted enum nickname; the domain is a type argument, so `Vendor<Hardware>` is the §2-horizontal \"one concept, every breadth\"). Counter-example done right: a CPU/DRAM model is a hardware *domain* model, not a universal framework, so its catalog *shape* lives downstream of std in `extdeps/\{cpu,memory\}` beside its cited vendor rows (`extdeps/cpu/ampere`) — std keeps only the frameworks they build on (`measure`, `types`); `compute_fabric` is a product-layer domain model. Likewise `std/os.dag` projects per-vendor rows with no `match` (\"projection only\"), while the cited rows and the product→vendor dispatch (`cited_operating_system_surface`) live in `extdeps/os/\{ubuntu,windows,macos,os\}.dag` (\"dispatch lives in extdeps/, not std/\", promoted from that hub header)."),
      li(text: "**§3 standing rule — cite the symbol, not the position (operator ruling, 2026-07-30): citations are symbolic / namespace-driven wherever a symbol exists.** A `file:line` pointer is a *second, positional naming scheme* for something the containment tree already names — the §3 nicknaming violation applied to prose, and it decays in a way the nickname does not: any edit *above* the cited line silently invalidates it, so the citation rots without anyone touching it or the thing it names. Measured cost that produced the ruling: three citation defects landed in one carrier (`extdeps.pin`) inside two days — two fabricated symbols (`review 44920`) and one stale line (`review 45088`) — and the stale line was then copied into `gunbc.design_document` as a row's supporting evidence, so a name-level grep passed while the located claim was false in the canonical design authority. **The rule:** name the module and symbol (`extdeps.pin` `admit_pin_integrity`, `v1_interpreter.rs` `observe_tool_identity`); add a position only where no symbol exists to name — a generated artifact, a data row, a line inside a blob — and where a position *is* carried, it is a convenience beside the symbol, never the citation itself. **Why this is stronger than diligence:** grep-verifying a symbol is exactly the `Node`-tree read the namespace authority already performs, so a stale *name* is decidable and lens-enforceable (`feature:cited-symbol-resolution`); a *line* is not reachable from that tree at all — a `.rs` offset is outside it — so the only mechanism that ever closes the positional half is to stop writing positions. The rule is therefore the cheap construction move and the lens is the residue, not the other way round (§5 construction-before-validation). `feature:cited-source-location-checked` retires when the corpus carries no positional citation that a symbol could have carried."),
      li(text: "**§3 convergence thread — fnv1a64 dual surface:** `v1.compiler.dag_collect_support.dag_node_surface_fingerprint_rec` calls the raw v1 intrinsics `atom_identity_hash`/`hash_combine` (fnv1a64 primitives registered in `04_method.dag` builtins); `std.content_hash` is the modeled surface over the same underlying authority. **LANDED (v2 carrier):** `v2.std.node` `Hash` is `std.content_hash` `Fnv1a64Structural` — the structural family member, NOT the subject-generic union; `content_hash`/`combine_hash`/`symbol_identity_digest` route through `std.content_hash`; `v2.std.materialize` keys on `ContentHash` (same identity as `RealizationPlan.target`). *(Corrected 2026-08-01, review on gunbc#7480: this clause read `v2.std.node.Hash = std.types.ContentHash`, which named the wrong home AND the wrong type once the family grounding row below landed — `ContentHash` no longer lives in `std.types` at all, and pinning the node carrier to the union would have re-opened exactly the cross-family comparison that row closes. The mechanism was always correct; the sentence describing it in this document was not, and it contradicted the Phase A row a few entries down — the §3 stale-citation class, caught by review rather than by the corpus.)* Remaining: ground `v1.compiler.dag_collect_support` fingerprint calls through `std.content_hash` when v1-stage v2 adoption clears."),
    ]),
    h3(text: "§3 standing rule — replacement migrations cut over at the root (operator ruling, 2026-08-15)"),
    p(text: "When two structures answer the same semantic question and one is intended to disappear, the change is a **replacement migration**, not an ordinary refinement. A replacement is not performed by gradually editing the old authority from its leaves upward until it resembles the new one. That approach preserves the old root while every intermediate state becomes observable and load-bearing: `X`, partial `Y`, adapters between them, comparison machinery, fallback arms, and consumers that understand both. Each intermediate state then acquires its own projections, witnesses, enrollment, compatibility decisions and eventual deletion — so the migration creates more authority before it removes any. The intuitive error is pruning a weed from the top while leaving its root in the ground: every cut changes the visible shape while preserving the mechanism that makes the unwanted thing return. And the ruthless half, ruled from live receipts (operator, 2026-08-15): **uproot the weed as early as you can, and fix forward minimally with the new solution** — because a surviving X is an *attractor*. Every agent session that enters the lane inherits its decomposition, studies its structure, unions its rosters, and preserves its parts, since deleting piece-by-piece requires first proving nothing depends on each piece; the weed's structure recruits gardeners. Worse, tip-first deletion is self-defeating by construction: every intermediate state must keep X alive and green, so the deletion program degenerates into maintenance — and then optimization — of the thing being deleted; you pay to improve what you are removing. Starting from the empty replacement inverts the burden of proof: nothing is in Y unless someone put it there, the denominator is explicit, nothing is carried because no one dared remove it, and nothing can newly depend on a thing that is gone. And the census itself is often the herring — measuring a structure you are about to delete is the attractor at work, enumerating what X *says* it provides instead of what is actually consumed. In a fail-closed substrate the **deletion is the census**: every real dependent refuses loudly — compile errors, unresolved references, red witnesses — so what breaks is exactly what was load-bearing (\"load-bearing\" is not intrinsic to a node; it is precisely the obligations that surface when the node is gone), and the deleter owns designing and fixing forward everything that actually breaks, until the tree is green again. What cannot break loudly — un-enrolled evidence, external consumers, coverage that was already silent — is the one thing deletion cannot discover, and it is covered not by a census but by the declared, bounded §4b rung-drop (previous rung, temporary rung, reason, restoration trigger — loud in the commit, never silent). gunbc#8146 is this shape merged: most of the old suite carried no load at all, and its deletion needed no solving. This follows from the sections above rather than being layered onto them — §2 prices an intermediate representation destined to be discarded as redundant work, §3 forbids X and Y both answering for one fact, and §5 rules that naming a dissolution condition does not authorize introducing the adapters and fallbacks that constitute the dual-authority period."),
    p(text: "The operative loop, compressed (operator sequence, 2026-08-15):"),
    CodeBlock { code: "1. back up to the root of the weed and delete it\n     -> the tree immediately surfaces both censuses:\n        the loads        (what depended on X)\n        the concealments (what X was hiding)\n\n2. for each literal compile error, solve from first principles:\n        implement the terminal solution in Y\n      | migrate the corpus (X as offline oracle)\n      | retire the need, with a receipt\n      | the error names another weed -> recurse to 1\n\n3. stop when the tree is green\n   and the declared silent residue is restored or retired\n" },
    p(text: "**What deferring the deletion costs** (the consequence catalogue, operator-directed 2026-08-15): while X stands, every nearby question is answered in X's vocabulary — the only operational terms present — so the organization is continuously forced into decisions premised on assumptions already scheduled for death. Four consequences, each strictly worse than the last: (1) **waste** — improvements to the thing being replaced die with it; (2) **propagation** — each dead-premise decision becomes a premise for the next: an optimized X's caches, schedules and vocabulary leak into Y's design space, so Y ends up negotiating with X's optimizations, and the dead assumption recruits dependents — removable debt converted into architectural constraint; (3) **evidence inversion** — an optimized X looks healthier, so each improvement reads as evidence the replacement is less urgent: §5's absorbing fallback operating at the architecture grain, the deficit's observed frequency zeroed by the very work that deepens it; (4) **unmarked premises** — a decision carries no tag naming the doomed assumption it rests on, so the eventual migration pays an archaeology cost to find and reverse premise-contaminated decisions — §1's deferred cost at interest, the stale-citation class generalized from prose citations to design decisions. The receipts paragraph below measures all four on the two bankrupted branches: the import-era completion and the coordinator re-fit are (1) and (2) caught live; that both branches read as progress while deepening the deficit is (3); the recut each then required is (4)'s archaeology, paid. Beneath all four is the axiom: A3 grounds agreement only on what stays stable across time, and a dead assumption is definitionally time-unstable — building on one is citing an authority already known to be revoked. This is also the frozen-X carve-out's real function: where X must keep running because no Y can hold the boundary, the freeze is not thrift — it **caps the premise supply**, so no new decision is grounded on the dying design. The same mechanism run forward is why delete-first needs no ongoing enforcement once performed (operator, 2026-08-15): with X gone its vocabulary is unavailable, so the cheapest next action and the first-principles action coincide — the existing approach stops being reachable, and solving the problem normally becomes the only move left. Hence the standing exercise that opens every cut: enumerate what is certainly scheduled for death already — the old pipeline step, the superseded grammar, the machinery only X consumes — and delete those files immediately, before any fix-forward work, so no further decision can be premised on them."),
    p(text: "**Existing designs are not substitutes for deletion** (operator ruling, 2026-08-15). A design document, plan carrier, or staged roadmap authored while X lived is itself one of the premise-contaminated decisions above: its terminal shape, area maps, and named obligations may be valid evidence, but its *sequencing* — hollow gradually, migrate then delete, delete only after prerequisite chunks — is X's gravity written down, reasoned inside the vocabulary the cut removes. On a cut, existing designs are re-read as quarry and the new approach governs first and foremost: the deletion proceeds on this rule's own sequencing, and a plan that would defer it is evidence about X, never authority over Y. Live specimen, same day as the ruling: the cli_run hollowing plan's chunk ordering was cited to defer that file's deletion minutes before the operator ordered it cut — the plan's area map survives as quarry; its sequencing did not."),
    p(text: "Replacement migrations are therefore planned **vertically from the root consumer**, never implemented horizontally by mutating the old representation layer by layer:"),
    ol(items: [
      li(text: "**Name the authority being replaced.** Identify the exact root consumer — or independently complete consumer population — whose answer currently comes from X. \"The parser\", \"CI\" or \"v1\" is too broad unless the exact consuming operation and subject population are named."),
      li(text: "**Trace one complete vertical.** Walk from that root through X to determine the minimum representation Y must provide to answer the same real question — consumer-backward design of one complete semantic vertical, never implementation-forward conversion of X's internal layers. (Traversal-order vocabulary is deliberately absent: the durable concepts are semantic closure, the consumer-relative root, the maximal envelope, and branch-and-bound on exact refusal.)"),
      li(text: "**Delete X's production presence as early as you can.** Once X is judged superseded, its production route, machinery, flags and dispatch come out — one deletion, not a sequence — as a declared bounded rung-drop; git history keeps the corpse as quarry and oracle, and what deletion removes is X's authority and its gravity. Two carve-outs bound \"as you can\": a boundary where the coverage gap is unacceptable (outward-facing interfaces, unrollbackable effects, safety floors others depend on) keeps the staged form — Y built in shadow, then one transition that switches the root and deletes X together; and delete-first presupposes a minimal Y able to hold the boundary *immediately*, even under-covering — where no Y can hold it at all (the v1 seed before any candidate image exists), X stays but stays **frozen**: no new investment, no completion work, quarry-in-place, because the attractor cost accrues either way — and frozen includes X's *growth surfaces*: a host-builtin registry, an escape-hatch arm, a compatibility table inside a frozen X accepts no new rows, because each row is a deferred modeling obligation the surface's existence recruited (the 2026-08-15 receipt: 38 of the interpreter's 125 host builtins had accreted into one driver file precisely because the surface accepted them), and a freeze that still accepts rows is not a freeze."),
      li(text: "**Fix forward minimally with the new solution.** Y starts as the smallest root that takes the boundary — the seed usually already exists — and grows only by earned additions: what breakage proves load-bearing, or what an explicit disposition retains; never \"X had it and I am afraid to drop it.\" The incoming problems are solved **one by one, each from first principles** (operator directive, 2026-08-15 — the general behavior, not a migration special case): every breakage is asked what the broken reference IS in the terminal model — a capability Y should provide (fix Y), a form the terminal model rejects (migrate the corpus, with X as offline oracle), a need that retires with X, or **another weed the error just named, in which case recurse: back up to its root and delete again** — and never restored by reflex to the old binding. And not every error is a load: deletion surfaces two censuses at once — what *depended* on X, and what X was *hiding* (an absorbing structure masks its deficits, §5, so their frequency was zero by construction until the mask came off; the import strip uncovered a declaration-registration gap that was never a load at all, and the floor deletion uncovered whole-tree defects the old path had absorbed). A load gets a disposition; an uncovered defect gets a root-cause fix in Y — it was always broken, X merely made it unobservable. Bulk restoration under a red tree is the attractor's last trick: each fix made \"the fast way\" is the old way, and X's decomposition rebuilds itself inside Y one convenience at a time. Work at class grain, not occurrence grain — one rule per diagnostic class, applied mechanically within it — but decide each class deliberately and in sequence. From its first increment Y has one executing root, one exact subject, a typed outcome and a discriminating RED, and every admitted component survives substantially unchanged into terminal Y — meaning its semantic authority and terminal consumer survive; its internals may be refined. X may serve from history or a pinned ref as an external differential oracle over the same subject; Y never calls X, falls back to X, or derives its answer from X — agreement is an observation about two independent answers, never an implementation technique."),
      li(text: "**Close the gap against green, never against X.** The rung-drop's restoration trigger is the tree green again — every enrolled test and gate passing through Y — plus explicit restoration or retirement of the declared silent residue deletion could not make break. Output-parity with X is never the bar: parity against X's shape re-imports X's decomposition. For the staged exception, admission is the identity join over dispositioned obligations, and the old route dies in the same PR that completes the join."),
      li(text: "**Keep the evidence for surviving claims, not the obsolete machinery — and not evidence whose only subject is retired.** Discriminating witnesses that establish an obligation Y still claims stay enrolled against Y; production machinery whose only purpose was to implement or compare X is deleted; and a witness whose only subject is behavior Y deliberately rejects is retired *with a receipt*, never left standing — otherwise tests become the mechanism by which obsolete behavior grants itself immortality. This is consistent with §4b's dissolution-on-climb rule and distinct from it: a climb keeps the RED because the claim survives at a higher rung; a rejection retires the claim itself, and its evidence follows it."),
    ]),
    p(text: concat("**The frozen-X carve-out above has one live qualification, and it is a status change rather than an exception hidden inside the word.** The carve-out is stated for an X whose replacement is advancing toward a bounded cutover; it is the wrong state for a production-critical X whose replacement was paused indefinitely. That is now the v1 seed's situation: gunbc#8352 closed unmerged and integration/v1-cut is quarry, so no bounded event retires v1 from the required-floor path, and \"no new investment\" against an unbounded horizon means paying an unbounded tax. v1 is therefore reclassified — semantics frozen, maintenance active — under a closed vocabulary of three admitted and five refused change classes, ordered so that any refusal dominates every admission, the refused list carrying exactly the growth surfaces the carve-out names. The authority is ",
      documentary_ref_symbol_text(reference: v1_maintenance_standing_ref),
      ", which also records the standing danger that an active-maintenance arm becomes an absorbing one if every proposal classifies as admissible, and states its own rung honestly: the vocabulary is consumed by review diligence, not by any gate, so it sits at mitigatable and is strictly weaker than the freeze it qualifies.")),
    p(text: "**A replacement root is consumer-relative, and supersession precedes translation.** A root is not a module or a top-level node; it is `exact consumer × exact subject population × exact semantic contract`. Before assigning descendant migration work, enumerate every *production* consumer of the old structure and map each exact consumer contract to a replacement root. When a replacement root satisfies an old root's complete contract — every admission and every refusal — and intercepts every remaining production path into its descendants, **those descendants are a deletion population, not migration subjects, and no corresponding Y representation is owed.** If `a → b → c → d` and `b1` satisfies the whole contract formerly produced by `b`, `c` and `d`, then `c1` and `d1` should not exist; building them is work on representations Y makes irrelevant. A descendant reached by any consumer *outside* that superseded path remains an independent root and is analyzed recursively — the analysis is a consumer-relative dominator question, and the deletion is licensed only when the new root dominates every remaining production path into the subtree. Evidence describing a superseded descendant's required behavior survives and is re-enrolled against the replacement even when the descendant's production representation is deleted. The reviewer's question, applied to every artifact a migration proposes: **will this be consumed substantially unchanged by terminal Y, or is a higher replacement root going to make it disappear?** If the latter, it belongs branch-locally as an experiment, in the quarry as evidence, or in the deletion census — never on the production roadmap."),
    p(text: "**And the census runs over dispositions, not raw edges: currently consumed ≠ required to survive — a consumer must earn survival.** Most of X's internal consumer edges are consequences of X's chosen decomposition; they say how X works, not what the future system requires. Things built *above* X may exist only to compensate for it — adapters, repair passes, fallback dispatchers, special diagnostics, orchestration workarounds — and if Y makes the underlying behavior unnecessary they are deletion population, not load. Before the cut is computed, every consumer-relative obligation is dispositioned against the terminal design — retained under the same contract, retained under a changed terminal contract, replaced by a named Y authority, reduced to evidence for a surviving claim, or retired with a reason — each retention naming the authority that justifies it: a surviving external interface, a surviving invariant, stored-artifact compatibility, an independently useful terminal consumer, a deliberate transition or rollback commitment. \"Something currently calls it\" is not a justification: **reliance establishes migration cost, never correctness.** The admission condition is two zeros, not one — `undispositioned consumers = 0` AND `uncovered surviving consumers = 0`: an undecided consumer blocks the cut but is never silently treated as retained, and a retired consumer needs no coverage at all. Three different things, never conflated: a **surviving obligation** (Y must cover it), **existing reliance** (measured and dispositioned), and **obsolete behavior** (Y should make it impossible) — confusing the second with the first is how compatibility layers become permanent. For an internal boundary under delete-first this census is not a prerequisite ledger — the deletion performs it (what refuses is what was load-bearing) and the dispositions are written by the fixes; the up-front form is owed only where deletion cannot be the instrument: gap-intolerant boundaries, frozen X, and the silent residue the substrate cannot make loud. The planning carrier is `gunbc.replacement_cut` (`ReplacementCut`, `ConsumerDisposition`, `admit_replacement_cut_plan`). The row cannot be authored without answering why the boundary is not higher (`HigherBoundaryAnswer` — the descended arm names its parent by `ReplacementCutId`, a joinable typed reference, never prose alone); the boundary census is explicit about emptiness (`BoundaryCensus`: enumerated-but-empty refuses as the ⊥-conflation, a genuinely consumer-free boundary declares itself with a basis); X's residual roles come from the closed `ExternalXRole` vocabulary — a forbidden role (fallback, internal resolver, production second opinion) has no constructor, and no-oracle-needed is a justified arm, never an ambiguous empty list; evidence is dispositioned on the same one axis (`EvidenceDisposition`: re-enroll, replace, retire with its reason, or `EvidenceUndecided` — visible parking that blocks admission exactly like a consumer's `Undecided`, since a deliberate deferral is still not a disposition against the terminal design). Plan admission refuses — typed, per-row, all causes counted — on any undecided row, uncovered surviving consumer, coverage or replacement naming the old root itself, duplicate consumer/evidence/region member, identical old and new roots, self-referential descent, an enumerated-but-empty external-role list, or empty covered region; and the verdict is sealed and bound — `AdmittedReplacementCutPlan` is a `sole_constructor` record whose one in-module mint is `admit_callers`-sealed to the admission fold alone, carrying the cut identity and a criteria digest over every plan field (`admitted_matches_plan` recomputes), so a forged or plan-detached admitted verdict is unwritable rather than merely discouraged. What plan admission deliberately does not establish — the authored census joined against an independently *observed* consumer population, obligation-grain coverage, executed parent-refusal receipts — is the migration-cut authority program; **no roadmap node is structurally registered to a cut yet**, and the typed roadmap projection is that program's future obligation, not a present fact."),
    p(text: "\"Divorced from X\" means **Y is semantically independent of X's authority**, not that Y rewrites stable primitives X happens to use. Y may freely reuse tokenization, parsing, `Node`/`Edge`, occurrence identity, the exact source inventory, and target-independent evaluation; X may serve — from history or a pinned ref once its production presence is deleted, or live only under the frozen-X and staged carve-outs — as a bootstrap producer, an external differential oracle, a mechanical corpus rewriter, or a historical measurement source. X may **not** be Y's fallback for unsupported cases, internal resolver, implicit provider, second opinion whose answer controls production, or a hidden implementation behind a Y-shaped carrier. And the minimum Y is not the smallest representation that executes the happy path: **it must preserve every required refusal** — a faster implementation that permits a binding the old one refused has erased a correctness distinction rather than completed Y."),
    p(text: "**\"Atomic\" describes the authority transition, not the amount of implementation work or the number of commits.** It does not mean one commit, one PR, no incremental development, or migrating the repository at once. It means Y has one coherent root; ordinary production never accumulates consumers that understand both X and Y; and X's authority ends in one motion. Under the delete-first default there is no shadow interval at all — the deletion is the transition, minimal Y takes the boundary immediately, and the declared bounded gap replaces the dual-authority overlap, trading visible temporary under-coverage for the overlap's invisible costs: two authorities, attention capture, shadow periods that empirically never end. The SCM vehicle is an **integration branch** (operator-directed 2026-08-15): a breaking cut never lands on main mid-loop — fork from main at a recorded base, make the root deletion the branch's first commit, and run the fix-forward loop there, where the tree is legitimately red on main's terms; main receives the cutover as one merge when the branch meets Y's green bar, so main never hosts a dual-authority interval and never sees the red. Work inside the region targets the branch, not main, and the branch inherits the freeze — it exists to build Y against the deletion's censuses, never as a third place to keep X alive. Where the staged exception applies, Y is exercised through a real shadow consumer while being built, and one accepted transition moves the root and deletes X's route in the same motion. When one cutover would be too large, partition by an **independently complete root consumer or cohort, never by internal layer**. A cohort is complete only when its exact identities execute through Y, are physically absent from X's schedule or dispatch, and have no fallback: migrating one exact witness population and deleting its old route can be a cohort; converting the lexer, then the parser, then the normalizer while ordinary execution still enters through the old root is the painful form. The cohort axis partitions subjects; the depth axis is governed by a **greedy root-first search: attempt the maximal cut, and descend only on an exact refusal.** The standing question is \"why can't we cut at the root — and why not higher\", never \"which deep module can we swap first\": the deletion envelope expands *downward* through X's implementation and *upward* through consumers, adapters, repair passes and workarounds whose only justification was X, stopping only at a contract with an independent reason to survive. There is no conservation-of-effort argument for defaulting deep: a separate production transfer at `c0 → c1` beneath a still-old `b0` pays admission surfaces, receipts, rollback boundaries and X-compatibility work the root cut never pays, so the maximal cut is usually the *cheapest* semantic plan even when Y's implementation work inside it is large. A refusal of the maximal cut is the only license to descend, and the refusal names where: **Y-incomplete** — fix Y and retry the same cut; the missing behavior belongs in terminal Y, not at a lower migration boundary; **an escaping consumer** — a live edge into the region from outside it; disposition that consumer: redirect it to a Y root, enlarge the cut set to replace it, or explicitly retire it; or **opaque** — bisect to *localize* the blocking obligation; bisection is the diagnostic fallback for a refusal that cannot name itself, never the migration order. A deep cut is legitimate exactly as the fallback decomposition of a refused root cut, under conditions: the lower boundary corresponds to an independently meaningful terminal-Y contract, carries every load crossing its region with no X-only adapter (an adapter needed to make the swap fit is the tell the boundary was not shared), immediately deletes a nonempty X region, and survives terminal Y unchanged — the lexer-then-parser ladder executed as complete swap-and-delete at such seams is this rule's staged *fallback*, never its default. The graph object is a minimum semantic cut over admitted surviving obligations, searched branch-and-bound: propose the maximal region, prove or refuse it, split only the refused portion, prune everything covered."),
    p(text: "Selective loading, affected sets, caches, batching, aliases and short names are **downstream projections of a correct Y, not prerequisites for establishing its semantics**. The first replacement deliberately chooses the simplest complete form — often the whole subject, canonical names, one manifest, one fold — and derives the smaller or faster projections afterward. A PR that introduces a new X↔Y adapter, production feature flag, dual-dispatch arm or fallback is introducing active migration debt, and per §5 that requires an explicit operator verdict on the exact introduced population; branch-local experiment remains unrestricted so long as the disposable machinery is deleted before merge."),
    p(text: "Every replacement migration names, at exact identity grain: the old root consumer and subject population; the new shadow root; the independent outer oracle; the cutover consumer; the exact production machinery deleted at cutover; and the discriminating evidence retained afterward. Its terminal receipt establishes:"),
    CodeBlock { code: "new root consumers                  = exact declared population\nold root consumers                  = 0\nundispositioned consumers           = 0\nuncovered surviving consumers       = 0\nproduction X<->Y adapters           = 0\nproduction fallbacks                = 0\nmigration feature flags             = 0\nunreconciled subject identities     = 0\nretained discriminating evidence    = exact declared population\nretired consumers and evidence      = exact declared population, each with a receipt\n" },
    p(text: "This rule does not prohibit incremental **refinement** — a change whose result remains the same single authority, survives the terminal architecture, and never requires a consumer to understand both representations. The test is not the size of the diff; it is whether the edit reduces the number of authorities or creates another one that must later be removed. **Receipts, in both directions.** The top-cutting shape, as read at the 2026-08-15 replanning review — dated measurements of then-open, still-moving branches, kept as the receipt of that reading and never as live status: gunbc#8080 fused native test execution, corpus migration and self-hosting while the displacement numerator stayed at 0/8,876 and the branch closed; gunbc#8115 had accumulated namespace machinery across 52 commits and >10,000 added lines while ordinary compilation still consumed the import-era path, the authored import population having grown to 18,628 lines across 2,884 files; gunbc#8244 had deleted the entry-major execution model but was fitting the new preparation concept into the old coordinator, plan, batch and policy structure across 88 commits and 250 files, with three plan-driven callers still demanding the subject separately. Both branches were BANKRUPTED on 2026-08-15 — frozen as quarry with their exact-identity machinery and controls retained as evidence, one having grown to the point of adding 351 import lines to make the import era internally complete — and recut as delete-first programs: the floor by deleting the CI step outright and re-adding obligations one by one from first principles, the imports by stripping every authored import line (19,173 across 2,955 files in the scratch measurement) and working the resulting typed diagnostics forward class by class. The careful branches died; their quarry survives. The root-cut shape: gunbc#8146 did not migrate 740 non-executing Rust tests one at a time — it selected the eleven-module executing kernel, deleted the rest, changed the authority from \"classify every departure\" to \"declare every resident\", left no compile-only fallback, and turned ~24,000 deleted lines into 56 tests that actually execute; gunbc#8182 removed one dead semantic family end to end across the CLI, primitive registry, generated dispatch and interpreter rather than deleting one layer and leaving adapters behind. And the delete-first ruling's own receipt is the 2026-08-15 required-floor session: successive agents were captured by the old floor's structure — roster unions, test relocation, instrumentation of machinery already judged ill-conceived — until the step was ordered deleted outright, obligations re-added one by one as breakage and dispositions earned them."),
    p(text: "The rule's first two cut programs are planned at file grain, on integration branches per the vehicle above: → [floor-cut replacement plan](docs/plans/floor-cut-replacement-plan.md) · [namespace-cut replacement plan](docs/plans/namespace-cut-replacement-plan.md)."),
    h3(text: "External upstream decomposition"),
    p(text: "“One concept, one authority” unifies a shared interface or formal shape; it does not merge independently governed entities that inhabit that shape."),
    p(text: "Each independently versioned upstream product, implementation, engine, vendor, or specification has its own extdeps module authority. Multiple source citations from the same upstream subject may ground that module. A generic hub may define or re-export agnostic shapes, but it may not enumerate concrete products, dispatch among their authorities, carry product-specific version rows, or store consumer coverage state."),
    p(text: "Observations produced by this repository are receipts in the observing product/workflow layer, not facts owned by the observed upstream. Missing observations are coverage obligations downstream, never `Unobserved` properties authored inside an upstream module."),
    p(text: "Example:"),
    CodeBlock { code: "extdeps.whatwg.html_navigation        shared standard\nextdeps.browser.chromium              Chromium implementation\nextdeps.browser.google_chrome         Chrome distribution\nextdeps.browser.webkit               WebKit implementation\nextdeps.browser.apple_safari          Safari distribution\nextdeps.automation.playwright         automation dependency\ngunbc.served_surface_browser_support  support policy and evidence join\n" },
    p(text: "A Chrome change must not require editing Safari’s module. Adding Opera must not widen a generic browser-product enum."),
  ]
}

fn section_4_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "4. The closed, grounded substrate (what makes §2–§3 decidable)"),
    p(text: "You can unify and decompose *mechanically* only in a closed, grounded system — so the substrate is built for it. A program is a dependency graph over two primitives (`Node` + `Edge`) and a closed vocabulary (6 connectives + 6 behaviors — `Value`, `Transform`, `Branch`, `Loop`, `Bind`, `Match`, per `v2.std.node.Behavior`; an earlier revision said 5, corrected against the live coproduct 2026-07-31 because the closed vocabulary is the denominator the decidability argument divides by); surface syntax is sugar that adds no power. Execution is **bounded and forward** (cyclic relations via acyclic encodings, never cyclic values; recursion is sugar over `Loop`), so decidability and termination *fall out* rather than being separately proved. **Grounding is intersubjective** — point at a shared framework, not an internal taxonomy — and in a closed system **a heuristic is never necessary**: the richer source always exists or can be written. This is *why* the structure is acyclic — a DAG in the substrate, serial reasoning in this document (recursively, per the preamble): intersubjective agreement holds only **across time** (§1), which demands claims that stay stable under it, so each is written syllogistically — a consequence-chain you can re-interrogate and that never wavers (algebra), never a cycle that could quietly redefine itself."),
    p(text: "Because the substrate is closed and grounded, the wins of §2 fall out for free: operations come from *inhabitance* (no per-type ops); and emission, ingestion, and coercion are **one** total decision procedure run in different directions (the epistemic chain *is* the emission algorithm — N models, not N×M adapters; every refusal a located, typed mismatch)."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "*e.g.* `dag/std/algebra.dag` derives `Int.add` from `Int` inhabiting a ring (ops aren't listed per type); idempotency dissolved from an `idempotent: Bool` flag into the `EffectShape` variant; termination is *checked, not discovered* — `DescentEvidence = Strict | NonIncreasing | DescentUnknown` inhabits a `BoundedLattice` with bottom = fail-closed."),
      li(text: "*e.g.* **one grammar, read in both directions** (§2 horizontal, not an adapter pair): ingest selects a production *forward* to fold surface syntax into a core `Node`; emit (`serialize_target ∘ translate`) selects from the *same* `target_model_edge_translation_rules` rows *backward* — the structural inverse, not a second emitter — so a new target language is rows authored in `extdeps/languages/`, never an edit to the fold (N rows, not N×M parser/emitter pairs). Coercion is that same move turned sideways: whether one model inhabits another is a homomorphism check (`coercion_fold` via `find_witness`), the same epistemic chain the emitter walks. One procedure asked in three directions, not three procedures."),
    ]),
  ]
}

fn section_4b_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "4b. Safety: the guarantee ladder (every error class climbs from runtime harm toward unwritability)"),
    p(text: "**Placed here because it is what §4's decidability buys, before §5 legislates the floor (operator directive 2026-07-31; worked examples and the rung census live in the recovery analysis linked below).** Safety is not the presence of diagnostics or the absence of crashes — it is the reduction of the state space in which a program can silently do the wrong thing. Every discovered error class sits on one ordered ladder and is obligated to climb it: **mitigatable** (the failure occurs; harm is contained by total operations, typed outcomes, bounds, rollback, isolation) → **mechanically preventable** (a generated test, lens, or gate reliably exposes and blocks it — but the invalid state remains writable, and safety depends on that mechanism executing and staying enrolled) → **structurally guaranteed** (the source can still describe the invalid state, but no `Accepted` program contains it: the compiler derives a proof or refusal from modeled structure) → **structurally impossible** (the invalid state has no constructor or representation in the canonical model — validation is unnecessary because the bad state cannot be written, and a refusal at the source boundary is then a symptom of the carrier's shape, not a separate check). The higher rung subsumes the lower — construction over proof, proof over validation, validation over mitigation; §5's construction-before-validation preference is this ladder's top two rungs, and its fail-closed doctrine is the law of the floor. Below the floor sits **silent wrongness, which is not a rung: it is outside the ladder and forbidden outright** (§5). Adjacent — and deliberately **not a fifth rung** — is **outside the modeled guarantee**: external reality, undecidable properties, undeclared intent. That column is observed, refused, or mitigated at a declared boundary, never fabricated (§5), and keeping it off the ladder prevents \"we do not model this\" from masquerading as a weak implementation that should climb."),
    p(text: "Every class carries an **attainable ceiling — derived, not aspirational**: a decidable, fully modeled class may reach structural impossibility; a decidable class missing its authority is §5's *wall after grounding*; a class blocked on a missing-or-unverified language capability names the capability as its trigger (the live one: **unforgeable construction** for carrier-borne invariants — `sole_constructor` is the existing candidate wall, a cross-module construction refusal with its own `SoleConstructorViolation` diagnostic and sealed-record specimen, but its completeness for generic refinement carriers, all construction forms, and the compiler-module check exemptions is **unverified** — the ceiling is an audit finding, not a declared absence; an earlier revision of this paragraph claimed no such capability exists, refuted by review 2026-07-31); an undecidable property honestly remains a ratchet or validator; an external fact remains a boundary obligation. **Below ceiling is a correctness gap, never optional elegance** — this clause is what keeps §6's purity trap from pricing out mandatory walls, the exact failure mode by which the recovered guarantee decayed unnoticed. Four meta-obligations make the ladder operational: (1) **rung honesty, at a declared subject grain** — the reported rung must equal the rung established by executed evidence, a discriminating RED refused on the real acceptance path plus an accepted positive control, **measured against a declared boundary** (source→interpretation, source→each emission target, phase-carrier→phase-carrier are different paths with independently different rungs: the interpreter refuses a mislabeled call that the emitter reorders into positional leftovers); a class's current rung is the **minimum across its in-scope paths**, or one row per structurally distinct path — citing the strongest path while another stays silent is inflation, and a type name, diagnostic variant, inert lens, or plan establishes nothing. **Rung inflation** (a carrier named for the top rung while occupying the bottom; a status ledger's \"DONE\") is the fabricated-plausible-output failure applied to the compiler's self-description — worse than sitting low, because an inflated class never ranks for climbing. (2) **no untracked stall** — a class below its ceiling must name its next-rung trigger, separating *cannot climb further* from *can climb after one grounding* from *can climb now but unbuilt*; only the first is permanent. (3) **no silent regression** — a change may lower a rung only by declaring previous rung, temporary rung, reason, bounded population, and restoration trigger; a compatibility exemption is not bootstrap glue, it is a visible safety regression with a finite runway. (4) **dissolution on climb — production handling only, never the evidence** — a climb deletes the redundant lower-rung *production* machinery it obsoletes (§2/§3: the proof that makes a state unwritable replaces the validator of the writable form, never accumulates beside it, unless that machinery independently guards an external boundary), but the class's discriminating RED and positive control **remain enrolled as the executing evidence that the higher rung stays real** — deleting them would recreate specification-without-execution one rung up; an expecting-red probe that greens when its wall lands flips to a permanent regression control, it does not retire (correction per review 2026-07-31: an earlier revision would have deleted the evidence with the machinery). Every newly discovered error class — incident, review finding, runtime exception, falsifier divergence — files or updates one row: invalid state, harm, distinguishing facts, rung found at, ceiling with reason, next trigger. That is how \"any class we find climbs\" is a repository rule rather than a cultural preference."),
    p(text: "**The floor first, the differentiator above it.** gunbc must first hold the ordinary compiler floor — names resolve, applications bind in exact bijection, values inhabit declared types, fields exist, closed variants eliminate exhaustively — and a failure there is a **below-baseline safety regression**, never compensated by higher-order capability. The differentiating claim begins above that floor: because the substrate carries causal, cardinality, algebraic, effect, ownership, cost, and realization facts structurally, the same ladder applies to classes ordinary compilers leave to tests, review, profiling, or production postmortems — a possibly-empty collection flowing into a nonempty consumer, recursion without a descent proof, a non-idempotent effect under automatic retry, a computation exceeding its declared complexity bound, operations whose declared algebra shows cancellation or duplication, a partially applied infrastructure change without convergence, a realization that does not preserve modeled behavior. The promise is **not** that every property becomes impossible; it is that every modeled class climbs to the highest honest rung its facts and decidability permit, with the ceiling and the residual risk explicit. Stated beside the ladder so a low ceiling is never mistaken for a gap: the compiler does not invent unstated intent (declared bound violated → refusal; declared algebra proves equivalence → derive; undeclared preference → never fabricated); does not prove unmodeled external reality (observe → typed evidence → admit or refuse → mitigate); does not lift arbitrary predicates to proof (the closed decidable cardinality fragment climbs where a general `fn(T) -> Bool` refinement cannot); and richer type names are not safety (a brand, wrapper, or `Validated<T>` is cosmetic until construction and acceptance enforce the distinction). Runtime mechanisms — typed refusal, totality, rollback, budgets — remain real and necessary at honest boundaries; they must only never be mislabeled as construction. The rung census over the recovered claims, the worked floor/differentiating/boundary examples, and the climb plan: → [compiler-guarantee recovery gap analysis](docs/plans/compiler-guarantee-recovery-gap-analysis.md); its Stage 1 lands the executable claims carrier (per-path disposition **derived** from executed measurements — no stored rung field exists, so a transcribed rung is unrepresentable rather than lens-caught — with ceiling + typed reason, next trigger, consumer-in-`Accepted`; corrected per review 45367, which caught this clause reading as a stored per-class rung column; the dispositions include `FrontierAccepted`, the typed-located-counted diagnostic whose phase result is still `Accepted` — distinct from silent below-floor AND from a refusal, per the 2026-07-31 post-merge verdict, whose live specimens are the open candidates' `MethodExistenceUndecided` and `GroundingNotDerived`), grounding the enforcement-intent thread below rather than minting a second registry beside it."),
  ]
}

fn section_4c_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "4c. Source annotations (prose the substrate can see)"),
    p(text: "**Source annotations are captured authored-source data, not semantic program data and not discarded trivia.** A language realization that admits annotation syntax routes it through an annotation-specific lexical channel; it may not produce an ordinary semantic token or namespace binding. Parsing returns annotations beside the semantic artifact, attached to eligible structural subjects. Annotation capture and provenance are disjoint from semantic occurrence allocation: adding, deleting, or moving an annotation cannot alter any semantic occurrence identity, semantic graph, resolution result, semantic hash, or target-program bytes. Textual provenance may move and must remain correct. Semantic compiler passes receive only the annotation-erased projection; source authoring, formatting, SCM, and annotation lenses consume and preserve the authored wrapper. The initial `.dag` realization admits only standalone leading `//` blocks attached to module-scope declarations; trailing, body, unattached, and block-comment forms refuse until separately modeled. Plain source annotations are modeling debt. Any invariant, receipt, event, ruling, citation, status, count, dissolution condition, or other machine-consumed fact belongs in a typed carrier. An ordinary `String` declaration whose sole purpose is commentary is misplaced or dead data."),
    p(text: "**Prose is not forbidden; unclassified prose is.** The rule exists because the opposite was tried and measured: removing `//` as a parse error (#5579) made comment *syntax* unwritable without making commentary unwritable, so the corpus adopted an explicit compensating convention — hoisting comments into `data …: String` rows (#6262) — where intent is mechanically indistinguishable from program data, and the first cleanup it forced swept 215 dead prose rows across ~130 files (#6424). That is the §2/§3 failure in its purest form: a second representation with no authority, undecidable by any lens. `//` is therefore the explicit quarantine boundary — the compiler keeps enough structure to migrate prose later without ever having pretended it was program data. Two consequences bind authors: an annotation may preserve irreducible human rationale about *why* a construction has its shape, and it must not restate what the declaration structurally says; and an annotation is never evidence that a machine claim holds, because no `Accepted` program can read one."),
  ]
}

fn section_5_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "5. Fail-closed (§1's safety axis — harm reduction)"),
    p(text: "Minimizing cost and complexity (§2–§4) is worthless if a wrong thing passes silently — this is §1's safety axis made concrete. This code is digital: a wrong answer is a **loud error, never a warning** — a bridge collapses, it does not warn. Every path succeeds fully or fails with a typed, located diagnostic; no fabricated plausible output (a bounded \"forever\" ≠ an \"unknown\" error). Relax toward application-layer leniency only under protest, and lean to infra so others can build on your work. Stronger than *catching* a wrong state is making it **unwritable** — **correctness by construction, not validation.** A check that re-states a constraint the model already carries is a *second representation* of it (§2/§3): so prefer a single authority from which the realization is *derived* — the bad state cannot be written — over a check that flags it after the fact, which concedes the bad state *is* writable. The tell that a check was validation standing where construction was available: it can be satisfied by editing the *declaration* while the realization still lies (a key-completeness check went green when the spec was edited while the realizer kept faking the cache key). Reserve post-hoc checks for the genuinely **unstructurable** residue (§6 complexity / necessity — you cannot structurally forbid an *unnecessary* loop). Construction makes a class unwritable only when membership is **decidable**, so every class is one of three: a *wall now* (decidable and grounded — nicknaming, dead scaffolds, effect leaks); a *wall after grounding* (decidable but waiting on its single authority — the cross-representation `==` straddle is one only once `Int = GroupCompletion<Nat>` grounds it); or a *ratchet forever* (undecidable — optimality, by Rice, never reaches \"never\"). The word **\"never\" is the trap**: it lets a ratchet masquerade as a wall, so check decidability before claiming one — the undecidable residue is the §6 lens, honestly and permanently. The deepest trap is **specification-without-execution**: a typecheck and a `.contains()` grep are *not* consumers — \"done\" means a real consumer **green by execution** plus a discriminating input that goes *red* when the behavior is wrong. (For the LLM agent: fluent, type-checking, grep-passing output is precisely the artifact that looks finished without running. Treat your own output as unverified until a consumer runs it green.)"),
    p(text: "The same demand for an independent referent governs a test's oracle (operator ruling, 2026-08-01). **A merge-blocking test may compare a live repository population to a numeric literal only when that literal is grounded in a controlled fixture, external/versioned authority, explicit policy budget, or a monotone debt contract over a closed subject universe. A measurement copied from the same current tree is not an oracle. Completeness is an identity join, not a count equality.** A controlled fixture independently authors the input and expected population (for example, one planted duplicate and an assertion of exactly one duplicate). An external or versioned authority names its source — such as a cited protocol arity, ABI field count, or content-addressed prior-release baseline; measured current main is not such an authority. A policy budget states the resource cap it enforces. A monotone debt contract is legitimate only when the subject universe is independently discovered and closed, membership is checked at identity grain rather than by count, the roadmap justifies the direction, and every removal or exception carries a typed disposition. Without all four conditions, a one-sided ratchet over hand-authored rows is only a quieter snapshot: it counts the subjects someone remembered to author. Review tell: if automating the literal's update collapses the assertion to `measure() == measure()`, the manual update was the test's entire content — a change detector, not a check. This rule rejects tree-copied census pins while preserving exact-count fixture tests."),
    p(text: "A third named trap, the subtlest because it wears this section's own name: **the absorbing fallback — degradation is disguised fail-open.** When a mechanism cannot compute its precise answer, the tempting failure arm substitutes the *superset* instead: can't compute the affected set → rerun the entire suite; cache key uncertain → scan all keys; provenance unavailable → always run. Nothing is *missed*, so the arm gets labeled fail-closed — but it is the fabricated plausible output one level up: **⊤-as-answer conflated with ⊤-as-ignorance** (state-space conflation on the answer lattice — \"everything is affected\" and \"I could not compute what is affected\" are different states whose remedies differ), and it fails open twice. On safety: absorption destroys the only signal that the precise mechanism has a deficit — the failure's frequency is zero *by construction*, so the deficit never ranks for fixing (§6 prices by displaced cost, and a masked cost displaces nothing) and the anemia compounds (the scan-all-keys heuristic in the receipt below was this exact absorption; removing it exposed 8 deficits its silence had hidden). On cost: the fallback is denominated in the *corpus*, not the *change*, so it grows with the repo until the *budget* breaks instead of the build — later, loudly, at interest: §1's safety debt paid in §1's cost currency. And the confidence **threshold** that selects such an arm is a smuggled heuristic — §4 already rules a heuristic never necessary in a closed system — so the threshold's very existence *locates* the anemic modeling it papers over: a solution that must keep degrading to stay green is not a solution being cautious, it is a model being wrong. The rule, and the review tell: **a failure arm must refuse, never widen** — every degradation a typed, located, *countable* diagnostic, so its frequency is observable and prioritizable, never an absorbed rerun. Two neighbors are not this pattern (check the *trigger*: structure vs failure-state): a structural over-approximation computed *as* the answer (a dependency-closure superset is the model's precision frontier, not an absorption), and a deliberate interim fallback that is loud, budget-bounded, and lands with its §6 dissolution trigger. Silent unbounded absorption is never the third. A corollary on the refusal itself: **no escape hatches** — a toggle whose only effect is \"proceed as if the refusal had not fired\" (an env var, a flag, a silence mode) re-opens the arm §5 just closed, one config edit from silent (operator ruling, 2026-07-05). The operative discipline is the **factory model — and it is a merge requirement, not a preference** (operator ruling, 2026-07-05): a deficit stops the line (typed, located refusal); the stopped line is analyzed before it restarts; the only sanctioned second mode is a *stopped-line audit* that replays the run to ledger every deficit (typed, located, counted) for that analysis — it reports, it does not green. Diagnostic modes read state; no mode writes \"keep going.\" **Review bar:** a diff that lands a non-fail-closed failure arm — a silent widen, a fabricated default, an uncounted degradation, an escape hatch — is a **hard reject** in code review, regardless of what else the PR delivers. The reviewer's three questions: does the line stop? is the stop typed and located? does analysis precede restart?"),
    p(text: "The same arm exists at authoring time: **the workaround — an absorbing fallback executed by the author** (operator ruling, 2026-07-24). When the obstacle is the substrate itself — a parse error you don't understand, a check that won't green, an interface that refuses — the tempting move is to route around it: a different spelling, a dodged codepath, a \"for now\". The concealment is identical to the runtime absorption above: the deficit's frequency is zeroed (nothing typed, nothing counted, nothing ranks for fixing) — except the concealed deficit is usually in the *language layer*, precisely the class §6 says to root-cause first. So the hard rule, in repo and in review: **noticing you are implementing a workaround IS the line-stop signal** — back up, reassess the problem, root-cause it or flag for help. A workaround is exactly an *unmarked* scaffold; what the marking buys is stated in the next paragraph, and it is no longer admission. Review tell: \"workaround\" / \"for now\" / \"dodge\" in a diff or PR body with no dissolution trigger beside it. Receipt: the `$\{…}`-in-strings dodge (2026-07-24) — a bare-`$` respelling shipped while the language already had the `\\\{` escape AND two real parser defects sat beneath the confusing error (a context-free diagnostic and a silent fail-open fallback arm); stopping the line surfaced all three within the hour."),
    p(text: "**A dissolution condition describes how admitted debt ends. It does not authorize creating the debt** (operator ruling, 2026-08-10). This supersedes, rather than supplements, the clause that formerly closed the paragraph above — *the only two landing states are the real fix, or a declared scaffold* — because in practice the trigger became a permit: *I know this is temporary, but I registered its future deletion, therefore it may merge.* **The default and expected landing state is the final construction. A scaffold is not an ordinary alternative to it; it is an operator-approved exception.** Any pull request whose current head introduces a net-new active dissolution obligation without an explicit operator verdict on that exact population is a **hard reject**: marked-but-unapproved debt is refused, and unmarked debt is the same violation plus concealment. The normal response to a proposed dissolution obligation is to back up and finish the underlying modeling, decomposition or generation work before using it; approval exists for exceptional cases, not as a routine route around that work. The rule follows from §2 rather than being layered onto it — redundant work spends someone else's future time for the author's present convenience, *including by building something destined to be thrown out* — so a throwaway artifact's cost is authoring plus review plus maintenance plus deletion plus review of the deletion, against a benefit bounded by its lifetime, and if the modeled path already exists the benefit is zero and the trigger was ceremony."),
    p(text: "**Lifecycle and admission are two questions, and the repository was conflating them.** *How would this temporary thing disappear?* is the generic lifecycle authority's: a bound condition carries a checkable trigger, an unbound one is honest prose structurally unable to report itself fired. *May we introduce this temporary thing at all?* is separate and project-level, because neither arm says the construction was justified — wrapping prose in an unbound condition beats letting prose impersonate a checkable trigger, and makes the underlying work no more sustainable. Likewise `std.disposition` `Scaffold` carries a construction mechanism and a declaration binding, and nothing else: no operator authorization, no proof the final path could not be built now, no benefit bought, no assessment of whether an already-modeled path was available. So the verdict is **not a Boolean**: *RejectAndFinishNow* (back up and build the sustainable path in this PR — the default), *ApproveTemporaryIntroduction* (this exact temporary construction may land), *AcknowledgePreexistingDebt* (the PR discovered and modeled debt already present on the base; it did not create it). Only the second admits **new** debt; the third exists so that documenting previously hidden debt never reads as creating it, which is the incentive that would otherwise keep it hidden. **Approval is external to the diff:** a row the PR authors itself is not approval — the author could write an approved flag as easily as the scaffold — so it is an *observed* fact (the configured operator, an APPROVED review, bound to the current head, invalidated by any later push, presented with the exact derived new-obligation set). And one obligation covers one independently removable unit: several artifacts share one only when they have the same terminal construction, fire on the same observation, and cannot survive one another — otherwise a single broad trigger launders an expanding collection of temporary work."),
    p(text: "**Out-of-band actuation has no ordinary scaffold arm** (same ruling; receipt gunbc#8073). Where the final path is *modeled intent → generated plan → modeled host-effect actuation* and the temporary path is *hand-authored YAML → embedded shell → host mutation*, the temporary artifact is not an early version of the final one — it never becomes it by refinement; it is deleted whole while the real path is separately authored. A hand-authored workflow, deploy script, migration script or manual mutation path that stands beside, bypasses, or will later be replaced by a modeled actuator is **manual application committed as source**: hand-writing an actuator is hand-applying it with a commit wrapped around it. Branch-local experimentation stays fully open — the canary may run, produce measurements, and be **deleted before merge**, leaving the modeled facts the final route consumes. Experimental work is not valueless because its implementation is disposable; it becomes waste when the disposable implementation merges into the long-lived authority and thereby imposes review, maintenance, interpretation and deletion work on everyone else. The mainline choices are the modeled path or nothing, except under an explicit operator ruling bound to the current head. Receipt: gunbc#8073 added three hand-authored Spark workflows beside the modeled convergence spine (`gunbc.fleet_converge_timer` → `gunbc.fleet_converge_apply` → `gunbc.host_effect_realize` `host_effect_apply`), one of them registering a `Scaffold` whose own note said the construction was *exactly the class the shell → intent routing forbids* — the trigger standing in for the decision. All three were deleted; the disposition had no subject once they were gone."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "*e.g.* a compiler-sized `.dag` corpus typechecked and passed its grep claims, yet `emit` hung >600s on `fn add` → rebuilt `emit = serialize_target ∘ translate` (43 lines), proven by *running* `emit(add)` against the literal `fn add(x: i32, y: i32) -> i32 \{ x + y \}`. Removing a scan-all-keys fail-open heuristic (`0331b526ee`) exposed 8 real inference deficits its fabrication had hidden; the parser's dummy `LitNull` nodes once fed inference bogus types. The absorbing fallback, formerly live in tree: `floor_witness_run_disposition` (`src/v2/workflow/affected_set_floor_runner.dag`) answered `DiffObservationFailClosed`, `FrontierFailClosed`, *and* the empty diff with `RunWitness` — one arm literally `\"empty diff — run full corpus (fail-closed)\"`, with tests *asserting the string* so the degradation was enshrined as the contract — and the corpus-denominated cost duly surfaced as CI's 90-minute timeout, not as a diagnostic. Repaired in two halves: the Building-&-checks enrollment inversion (the loud half), then the arms converted to typed refusals — `Refused` with per-cause variants, per-row counted, siblings continue — with the empty diff dissolved into the general disposition, never a special arm (operator rulings, 2026-07-05)."),
    ]),
  ]
}

fn section_6_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "6. How to work (given §1–§5 — these coexist)"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Model:** DFS the concept DAG before inventing vocabulary; fact-bundle modeling (invent or reuse on proven coincidence, never bare-alias); a finished stage is one fold (any non-fold residue is either a named irreducible kernel or un-migrated modeling — there is no third); model just-in-time and let the mark on the carrier be the authority (no parallel-ledger docs); **no scaffold lands by author declaration alone** — every net-new scaffold is represented by one exact dissolution obligation, joined to an external operator verdict on the current head, and refused in its absence (§5); reviewers independently search for undeclared scaffolds, so the author's classification is evidence to inspect, never the denominator."),
      li(text: "**Intellectual sustainability:** don't spend future author, reviewer, operator or maintainer time to buy present convenience. Work expected to be thrown away is *presumed redundant* (§2), so back up and complete the construction that will survive; use a temporary one only after the operator explicitly chooses that future cost. The reviewer's independent test, applied whether or not the author labelled anything: **will this artifact survive the terminal architecture substantially unchanged, and be consumed by it?** If no, presume scaffold and stop the merge until the final construction lands or the operator approves the exact exception. A *missing* dissolution condition makes the finding **more** severe, not less — the response is to name the undeclared dissolvable concept, request the final architecture, and require the exact obligation model if the author believes an exception is warranted; adding a trigger after review does not resolve the objection, it only makes the proposal eligible for a decision. Automated detection can never be the whole policy, because temporary work can be authored without using any of the names a gate matches on. Tells, each carrying a presumption: a hand-authored workflow, deployment script, migration script or operational command (out-of-band actuation); a second path beside an existing modeled route (parallel authority); \"bridge\", \"shim\", \"compatibility\", \"for now\", \"temporary\", \"until\", \"later\" (deferred refactor); a model to be deleted whole when the real one lands (throwaway model); a hand-authored projection the model should generate (manual application committed as source); raw shell implementing semantics already expressible in `.dag` (unmodeled realization); a broad wrapper around a type or modeling deficit (workaround hiding substrate work); a condition whose terminal is \"rewrite this properly\" (the proper work was not done); a new artifact with no final consumer (experimental residue). If dissolution approvals become frequent, that is not a process cost to optimize away — it is evidence the repository is routinely borrowing future intellectual labor to ship present convenience."),
      li(text: "**Prioritize holistically, not by the bottleneck:** balance the quantitative and the qualitative — don't anchor on one KPI (you'll hit it at a cost) or on pure taste. Map the cause→effect across sections; a 5ms step doesn't get a pass for not being the 80s one (it might be a 5ns step). One consequence is a standing rule, **bare minimum cost** (operator ruling 2026-07-10): a proven cost-shape defect — a copied accumulator, a quadratic fold — is *always fixed*, regardless of the realized n. \"n is small here\" is not a time-stable fact (§1/A3 — reuse changes n), and pricing per-site exceptions is itself redundant work (§2); the humility is not trusting your own \"negligible here.\" Root-cause to the language layer and fix related systems *together* — a local subsystem patch is the forked-logic trap. **Denominate the benefit:** the deliverable is a *displaced cost* (§1's time — a pain someone pays to remove); the lens/substrate is the *mechanism* (the moat), not the product. A lens — or any construction wall — is on-dial exactly insofar as it is the cheapest path to such a pain. Priced in elegance instead, the work is self-referential and unbounded (the purity trap — the economic twin of \"never\" in §5; an extensible substrate's infinite improvability dissolving its own bound)."),
      li(text: "**Enforce with lenses,** not grep — but **construction first** (§5): a lens is *validation* (it concedes the bad state is writable), so make the class unwritable by single authority where you can and reserve the lens for the unstructurable residue. As a residue mechanism it earns its keep: a pure reader over the same `Node` tree, storing nothing, so a new analysis costs zero substrate edits. Beware the tier where the machinery exists but nothing gates on it — coverage by illusion; an inert lens is itself a lie. **The corpus-wide backstops that used to police this are DELETED (2026-08-11), and the reason is the rule that replaces them:** the inert-lens reach census and the construction-justification census both ran inside floor witness discovery, so *every* discovery run — on every PR, on regen, in every coordinated worker — had to acquire a whole-corpus module graph in order to answer a question about who authored a lens. That is the §6 cost-shape defect at its purest: the unit of computation was the world, the unit of fact was one module's authorship, and the price was paid by every consumer that wanted a witness roster and nothing else. **Who paid it, split by era, because this document must not assert a superseded population as the present one:** before #8140 the walk was unconditional, so ordinary CI, regen, the falsifier cadence and every coordinated worker all paid; #8140 made it demand-directed, so a discovery-free plan such as regen stopped paying; from #8140 to this deletion the two censuses burdened every remaining discovery-bearing execution. The correction is recorded rather than reworded because a change deleting stale supply-side enforcement must not land a fresh stale assertion in the same diff (review on #8141). Neither census had a consumer that justified the acquisition; the inert-lens half additionally reported through two host builtins whose `.dag` surface was a pair of self-recursive stubs (`fn f() { f() }`) reachable only because the interpreter intercepted them. **What is NOT claimed:** this is a real scope narrowing, not a climb. A newly authored lens with no witness, and a lens recording no `construction_justification`, are both writable again and nothing detects either. The obligation survives as review diligence, which is strictly weaker. **Next-rung trigger:** an authorship fact belongs on the module's own declaration, checked at ingestion where the module is parsed anyway — one module's facts from one module's source — rather than reconstructed corpus-wide by a consumer that wanted something else. Until that lands the class sits at *mitigatable*, declared here rather than left to be rediscovered."),
      li(text: "*e.g.* one catamorphism `fold_node` is reused by all 7 v2 stages; #4699 dissolved `06_translate` 4,912→3,973 lines (`_go` accumulators 35→0); a 6-line `merge_envs` root fix cut reconcile from 81% of the pipeline to 6% (~2× self-compile) — the symptom recurs wherever the root is unfixed (v2 still hand-rolls `ParseTable` because the Realization carrier is staged, not inhabited)."),
    ]),
  ]
}

fn section_7_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "7. Self-hosting (the principles applied to the compiler itself)"),
    p(text: "The compiler is a pure transform and an ordinary substrate fact, analyzable by its own lenses. It is written in itself: the `.dag` graph is the truth and Rust is one realization — a seed that shrinks toward zero. v2 emits its own sources as the **cleanest principled realization**, proven **by execution** — the emitted module compiles and is **behaviorally equivalent** to the seed on a discriminating corpus (green-by-execution + a discriminating RED, §5) — **not** by byte-matching the seed. A byte-identical fixed point is explicitly *not* the goal: it is a quality drag, forcing v2 to reproduce the seed's warts to match bytes and thereby cementing poor decisions (§1 — spending future time to preserve the present seed's accidents). The seed shrinks across a **typed self-host frontier**: each module is either *self-emitted* (green-by-execution) or *seed-retained*, and a seed-retained module is a **declared row with a reason and a migration trigger** — countable, prioritizable — never a silent escape hatch (§5; the `DecodeFidelity`/`Lossless` boundary, §4). Its tests are data. Its ontology dissolves into `std/` — no dual representation at the compiler/user boundary. This is the recursion: every principle above governs the system that implements them, and this document."),
    p(text: "The recursion's payoff is that **language design itself opens up.** It is normally locked — not by lack of will but by cost: a new check means owning a *compiler fork*, and a new language means an *adoption* problem. Both are dissolved here at once: a wall is a **row** (§2, no fork), and because the substrate is medium-agnostic — one grammar read in both directions over many media (§4) — that row applies *on top of an existing language* (no adoption). So language design collapses (compiler-fork × language) → (row + medium): a domain's bug-class can be made extinct in Rust or TypeScript without forking their compilers. It is sound exactly where ingest is `Lossless` and **fail-closed** where it is not (the `DecodeFidelity` boundary, §4) — \"any language, with a typed honesty boundary about where the wall holds,\" not a vibe. This is §1's *reduce convention to necessity* at the meta-level, and the place the purity trap bites hardest: bounded by §5 (\"never\" = decidability) and §6 (priced in displaced cost, not elegance)."),
    hr(),
  ]
}

fn failure_modes_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "Recurring failure modes (instances of §3–§5, kept for pattern-matching)"),
    p(text: "hollow alias (minimality ≠ grounding) · state-space conflation (an `Option`/`None` meaning >2 things — split into named variants) · absorbing fallback (a failure arm that widens — rerun everything, scan all keys, always run — instead of refusing; ⊤-as-answer conflated with ⊤-as-ignorance; the deficit's frequency zeroed by construction, the corpus-denominated cost breaking the budget later) · **empty-observation narrow** (the absorbing fallback's mirror: ⊥-as-answer conflated with ⊥-as-ignorance — an observation that could not express what changed rendered as the verdict \"nothing is affected\", so the run narrows to nothing instead of widening. Strictly worse than the widen §5 already forbids: a widen is merely expensive, a narrow is silently uncovered. Live specimen: a push whose baseline ref *is* the pushed ref diffs the commit against itself, and the witness floor reported the resulting zero-path observation as \"N unaffected (import-closure, skipped without resolve)\" — receipt, two main pushes reporting an identical `4118 unaffected` on different commits, a skip count that cannot be a function of the diff. The tell that one observation is wrong rather than three local choices reasonable: its consumers disagree — compile-clean widened to whole-tree, regen gated on the event name, the witness floor skipped) · cache impurity (key on declared-input content; byte-identical cached-vs-cold is the purity oracle) · reflection evidence ≠ structural proof (prove a read axis by execution, with a no-host-enumeration control) · coercion proven by normalized round-trip, not a golden string · parallel-representation debt (an honestly-marked scaffold duplicating a canonical fact is still a violation) · internal review finds missing tests, external review finds missing checks · unmarked workaround (an author-side absorbing fallback — routing around an obstacle with no dissolution trigger; the line-stop signal, never a landing state) · **self-authorized dissolution** (temporary work treated as admissible because the author named its future deletion — a trigger is a lifecycle fact, not permission; without an operator verdict, marked debt is rejected exactly like unmarked debt) · positional citation (a `file:line` pointer where a symbol was available — see §3)."),
  ]
}

fn open_threads_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "Open threads"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "`v2.std.determinism` — **mechanically preventive for uniquely named registered roots; not repository-wide structural determinism** (honest rung correction, 2026-08-01). Determinism is emergent and boundary-relative (built on `std.perturbation`; a leak carries a `DeclarationRef` to the root reached, not a label enum), and `v2.lens.determinism.determinism_compile_gate` — enrolled in `v2.compiler.compile` `always_required_root_lenses` — refuses the two uniquely named Map projections whose traversal order is host-unspecified: `map_keys` and `map_values`. The former mechanism registered only `map_keys` and asserted it was the one readable nondeterministic atom; that premise was false because `map_values` exposes the same host iteration and was silently classified deterministic. The generic aliases `keys` and `values` remain undetectable: the lens sees only an unqualified callee `Symbol`, v2 `Node` carries neither receiver domain nor resolved declaration identity, and method/free-call lowering erases the distinction; registering either symbol would wrongly refuse unrelated same-named functions. Absence from the root list is therefore **not evidence of determinism**, absence of a diagnostic does not establish that no order-exposing operation was reached, and a newly introduced but unregistered operation is not detected. The attainable ceiling is explicit: the primitive subject universe is forked across `v1.compiler.infer_method` `builtin_function_registry`, `std.primitives` `PrimitiveContract` rows, `std.algebra` templates, native `v1_interpreter` dispatch arms, and per-target emit handlers. This is reachable rather than theoretical: `fold_list` is abundant in compiled v2 trees and has a native interpreter arm while appearing in neither the algebra Map surface nor the builtin registry; `index_by` has a writable native map-producing arm and a `PrimitiveContract` while likewise outside both. A closed denominator cannot be assembled by adding another partial roster. Dissolve-on: the `PrimitiveDefinition` identity join closes the denominator, disambiguates aliases, and gives roots resolvable identity by naming invocation forms, receiver domain, parameters, result, realization, and cost once; then every operation has exactly one traversal-order classification, unclassified refuses, and unsafe roots derive from that one authority. The x2 sampling emit gate remains deleted (#6551 · #6576 · #6594); authority is the carrier (`dag/std/determinism.dag`, `src/v2/lens/determinism.dag`), not this receipt. Prefer preserving meaningful source order in a separate list while using a map only for membership (the corrected PR #7515 pattern); there is no universal `sorted_map_values` order."),
      li(text: "**witness cost derives from purpose — a test is not entitled to arbitrary computation merely because it is a test** (operator ruling, 2026-08-04). A test's size must be DERIVED from what it proves: a modeled boundary it crosses (the smallest crossing specimen is the requirement plus one, never a realistic-looking input chosen for verisimilitude); a live population whose breadth is the subject (which decomposes into small discriminating mechanism fixtures per PR plus the irreducible census on an enrolled cadence — the census may be irreducible, the mechanism never is); an external effect that IS the subject (wet, under an explicit budget); or a resource contract whose threshold comes from a declared requirement. Anything else large is a benchmark, a soak test, a demonstration, or a cost-shape defect in the implementation, and none of those may gate. **The specimen that produced the ruling** is §5's absorbing fallback executed by an author, at the admission layer: a required per-PR witness went over the five-second fast-lane budget, its file was moved under `test/claim/long/`, it left per-PR discovery, no cadence row was added, nothing executed it, and admission still held — green CI, zero executed coverage, source file left behind. That is not deferral; it is deletion of the evidence with the file retained (gunbc#7762 commit `0cfd199f8`, thirteen witnesses, titled *fix CI fast-lane budget failures*). The root was a **directory deciding an admission question**: `OfflineLocalRecipe` — the verdict a broad path pattern produces — was accepted unconditionally, while the exact-admission authority already refused that same shape on an admission row. Two corrections fell out of it. **A claimed construction backstop must be checked against the transformation it is supposed to prevent, not against the state it describes**: `long/` was blessed on the ground that the eval deadline makes an over-budget witness unwritable-in-place, and the deadline is evaluated only while a witness RUNS, so relocation — the exact move — disarms it. And **`BudgetExceeded` is an interruption plus a measured lower bound on cost, never a semantic verdict on a witness, and never discharged by causing its relocation**. **Landed — stated at the scope it actually has**: path location is retired as *sufficient admission for a NEW witness*, and the legacy path-only debt is frozen and counted. `long/` remains an organizational home until that population reaches zero. Mechanically: admission splits into two independent axes because *allowed to remain* and *has an executing consumer* are different facts and one success arm cannot carry both — `WitnessExecutionStanding` (`WitnessHasExecutingConsumer\{cadence}` | `LegacyFrozenPathDeferral` | `UnclassifiedPathDeferral` | `UnexecutedDeferredWitness`) and `FrozenBaselineStanding` (`Allowed` | `Shrank` | `Grew` | `Stale`). The **coverage** gate admits only the first arm, so the frozen population never reads as covered and a downstream *is this behavior exercised* receives no; the **floor** gate additionally tolerates `LegacyFrozenPathDeferral` while the baseline axis holds, so the tree stays green through the migration without anything claiming those rows execute. `gunbc.witness_deferral_freeze` enumerates the legacy identities at identity grain (701 across 152 entries at the freeze point); anything outside it and outside `gunbc.explicit_witness_admission` refuses, a frozen row the tree no longer carries refuses, and the roster may only shrink against its diff baseline. The seed realization in `cli_run.rs` is a registered `Scaffold` with a measured landing delta and an ordered dissolution — a modeled policy does not exempt its realization from the hollowing plan. **Open**: the cost half — a per-witness declared cost envelope, which is the trigger for the modified-in-place residue (identity grain cannot see a frozen witness whose body grew a corpus scan) and the condition on which the freeze, the path-policy pass-through arm and the `long/` dir row dissolve together."),
      li(text: "model §1's axioms (A1–A3) explicitly in `.dag` and have a lens **enforce the syllogism** — every claim a consequence-chain back to an axiom, no orphan and no cycle (the §4 acyclicity test turned on the argument itself; the §7 recursion, with this document as the first target). (operator's next project) → candidate articulation for review: [intent-linearity draft](docs/plans/intent-linearity-design-draft.md)"),
      li(text: "**enforcement intent — ask once, compile forever.** The operator's recurring standing directives (enforce complexity repo-wide; lenses must be live; lenses must self-apply; scope must not silently narrow; model must not carry dual representations; a failure arm must refuse, never widen — no absorbing fallbacks, §5) are redundant governance work (§2) re-paid each conversation — the operator performing the missing meta-lens by hand. Model each as a durable `StandingIntent` row and gate the relationship (intent ⇄ `LensContract` ⇄ coverage receipt) fail-closed: a mechanism *claiming enforcement* is complete only when it satisfies the `StandingIntent` — named live consumer, declared scope not narrowed, red control, self-application or explicit exemption — else Unknown/Refused, never silently green. One new authority (`StandingIntent`); everything else extends existing machinery — the registry (`LensRegistryEntryV0` → `LensContract`), reusing `ConstructionJustification` / `subject_roster` and consuming `intent_linearity` / `self_applying_lenses` for the fractal (§7) layer (the same property applied to the code, the lens, the subject producer, the registry, and the acceptance template). Default enforcement scope = whole corpus; under-scope is a failing receipt unless explicitly justified. → [enforcement-intent design](docs/plans/enforcement-intent-design.md)"),
      li(text: "can a lens mechanically diagnose the *leaf-side* of decomposition (§2)? (operator-parked)"),
      li(text: "**hollow-alias construction wall — DRAFT, design-note-first (sharp-bee-290 mandate).** A bodyless (`PhantomData`-collapsing) declaration reached by construction is a §5 unwritable-class candidate, not a validation check: `GroupCompletion<M>` (PR #7197, infra-blocked) and `FieldOfFractions<R>` (PR #7210, record body \{ num, denom \} content-reviewed clean pending CI + approvals) are two grounded specimens, neither merged to main yet; a third, the emit-side companion, is the `checkpoint_scalar_phantom` class — a checkpoint scalar (arity 0 in Rust) reached with a phantom type arg (`GroupCompletion<Nat>`, `Compose<Int, MachineWidth<>>`) cannot legally render as `i64<T>` (E0109), documented in-code at `src/v1/stage0/src/v1_compiler_emit_rust.rs:746` and converging with vivid's ~50-finding `checkpoint_scalar_phantom` E0107 bucket and loyal-raven's dotted-path `GroupCompletion<v2.std.nat.Nat>` E0308 finding — tracked as Root-4, separately gated, not this note's scope. This note's own rule: bodyless + construction-reached ⇒ refuse; bodyless + `= Node`-aliased (the existing 11-occurrence declared-abstract idiom) ⇒ legitimate; bodyless + never construction-reached (pure type-tag, e.g. `Hardware`) ⇒ legitimate. Sequencing is lens-first (a pure structural `Node`-tree reader, not grep — the doc's own census section is a case study in why grep false-positives ~1,739 hits), promoted to a typechecker refusal only once proven zero-false-positive corpus-wide. No code lands from this note yet. → [hollow-alias construction wall](docs/plans/hollow-alias-construction-wall.md)"),
      li(text: "the model↔realization fork is systemic, and where unfinished it fails open: every primitive is modeled as a coproduct and realized as a native `Value`, reconciled by per-site bridges, so coverage is accidental and non-compositional. `match` bridges `Int→Zero/Succ` but `Value::eq` has no `Int↔Variant` arm, so `nat_add(85, 32) == 117` silently compared `false` at its `_ => false` chokepoint — a §5 fail-open, not the §2/§7 redundancy the 🟡 dissolve-on markers track. **Landed:** the *numeric tower* fork now fails closed — `eval_binop`'s `BinOp::Eq`/`Ne` raises `InterpError::CrossRepresentationEquality` when a `false` result is *explained* by a native `Int`/`Float` vs `Nat`-coproduct straddle (recursive: catches `nat_add == nat_add` and `[nat_add(1,1)] == [2]`), with `Value::eq` left infallible so it stays the single `CanonKey` map-key authority. The discriminating witness WAS `cross_representation_equality_test` (forks → typed error; reconciled/native → `true`; genuine diffs `1 == 2`/`Succ\{..\} == Zero` → `false`, not error), DELETED 2026-08-11 by the gunbc#8146 v1 Rust test-suite cutover. Note what that deletion did and did not change: the module was gated by `--no-run`, so those assertions had not executed since that gate landed — the receipt was already nominal — and the guard itself is untouched and still live in `eval_binop`. The guard is now UNWITNESSED; dissolve-on: re-establish the assertions as a floor witness through `compile_dag_diagnostic_census`, or retire the guard with the `Value::Null` split. **Remaining:** (a) the same straddle for `Bool True|False` over `Value::Bool` (no `==` site in the corpus today) and `Optional/Witness` over `Value::Null` — the latter resists a blanket guard because `Value::Null` is the overloaded `None`/`Absent`/miss sentinel and `present == None` (131 sites) is a *legitimate* `false`, so it needs grounding, not an error arm; (b) the root fix (§1/§2/§7) — ground each primitive into its realization. **Numeric tower: GROUNDED** (#5428, 2026-06-21) — Nat construction-side grounded (`Zero → Int(0)`, `Succ\{prev:Int(k)\} → Int(k+1)`); native form == modeled form; `eval_binop` `CrossRepresentationEquality` guard is dead-in-corpus for numerics, kept as fail-closed backstop until the `Value::Null` split lands (guard removal bundled with that work, fenced out of this window). **Remaining:** `Value::Null` split — Optional/Witness/miss into own carriers (~131 sites; the deeper root, its own runway). Phase A (plan + witnesses, #7261); carrier split not landed. → [value-null split plan](docs/plans/value-null-split.md) (operator: `==` fail-closed, 2026-06-20) · **Next layer (PR #7197, open, not yet merged):** `dag/std/algebra.dag:38`'s `GroupCompletion<M>` is hollow (bodyless — no fields at all), forked a second time by `src/v2/std/integer.dag`'s independent redeclaration — the emitted-Rust operator-trait surface #5428 left uncovered renders it as `PhantomData`. PR #7197 grounds `GroupCompletion<M>` as the Grothendieck pair construction (`\{pos: M, neg: M\}`) at its single authority, deletes the v2 duplicate in favor of importing it (§2/§3), collapses a native-`Int`-fielded pair construction in `eval_record_lit` (mirrors #5428's `Succ\{prev\}` collapse), and swaps the emitter's zero-param alias-decl branch to the single-authority `rust_scalar_checkpoint_render_base` so the `Int → i64` checkpoint fires under both corpus representations. Content-reviewed clean; CI in progress. → [GroupCompletion pair-construction design](docs/plans/groupcompletion-pair-construction-design.md)"),
      li(text: "**§3 residue — ContentHash family grounding — Phase A LANDED (#7480).** `std.content_hash` grounds `ContentHash` as a family coproduct (`Fnv1a64 | Sha256Hash | Sha1Hash`) with where-refined digest carriers; union-level `content_hash_eq`/`combine` deleted; `compare_content_hash` returns `ContentHashCrossFamilyIncomparable` (third state, not Bool collapse). Carrier walls: `OciDescriptor.digest` → `OciContentDigest`; `GateRosterHash` / `v2.std.node.Hash` → `Fnv1a64Structural`; Git tree hashes → `GitObjectId` (Sha1, cited via `extdeps.git.object_store`). `extdeps.crypto.hash` `sha256_digest_content_hash` is the VALIDATING PARTIAL bridge `Digest`→`ContentHash?` (invalid hex refuses via `Absent`, never absorbs a fallback), and `std.content_hash` `as_content_hash_cryptographic` is the TOTAL injection from an already-validated `Sha256Digest` — the distinction matters because only the second is unconditional (corrected 2026-08-01: this row previously called the partial bridge total, and its positive-path witness was named `..._is_total` while a sibling test proved the same function refuses invalid hex); `sha256:` prefix is wire serialization (`serialize_content_hash`), not a discriminant. `Pin.expected_identity` retains the union (subject-generic); `admit_pin_integrity` routes cross-family to `CrossFamilyIdentityIncomparable`. R0 backstop refuses bare union `==`/`!=` on family mismatch (`content_hash_cross_family_eq_v1_seed_bridge_note`; witness `cross_representation_equality_test`, DELETED 2026-08-11 with the gunbc#8146 suite cutover — the backstop is live and UNWITNESSED, dissolve-on recorded on that note). **Remaining Phase B (dissolve-on — this row deletes when landed):** substrate typecheck refuses union-level `ContentHash == ContentHash` so cross-family comparison is structurally unwritable; Phase A closes carrier construction walls + R0 eval only. Authority: `std.content_hash` `content_hash_family_grounding_note`. Witness: `dag/test/claim/content_hash_family_grounded_witness_test.dag`, `where_refinement_enforcement_witness_test.dag` (hex refinement at literal sites)."),
      li(text: "the remaining deleted-`docs/` references in `.dag` comments — provenance / `bind:` pointers into the bankrupted `docs/` tree (e.g. `docs/planning/*`, `design-*.md`) — fold into the dep-graph reform, not a blind repoint. (The named-corpus ledger marks — `Practice N`, and `INVARIANTS` / `THESIS` / `MODELING` / `RELEASE_TODO` / … citations — were swept: dropped, or re-homed to DESIGN.md §-anchors.)"),
      li(text: "floor shared-computation memoization — M1 entry-closure memo LANDED (#6999, merge dc2aa25684); post-merge validation (msg_1879f052) shows ~0% batch-wall recovery on capped hosts — fixes are mechanism-correct but discovery loads each entry once/worker; residual ~18min owed to #6848 once-per-entry bare-reference fixpoint + cap-saturation throttle (§1.4). Receipts: [floor-time namespace-walk diagnosis](docs/plans/floor-time-namespace-walk-regression-diagnosis.md) §5. **BANKED:** slice-1 measurement (lane `ci-cost`, subject `entry-graph-union-construction`) merged as #7483 (b5191426479ddec5f9a80b60dc6264ca168b42b6), landed a partition mechanism and one corrected finding, and RETRACTED three claims an earlier draft of this row asserted. What stands: the `[resolve-split]` rows were never quotable as shares because children were summed over every resolve a thread runs while the receipt's parent covered only witness-entry resolves — two denominators, not a nesting — and an exclusive partition over a parent that contains its children now reconciles at 0ns tolerance, refusing (`OverAttributed`/`NestedSpanAttribution`/`NoSpans`) rather than clamping; and the production-selected closures repeat module membership at a 35.9-38.4x duplication factor across narrow/typical/broad subjects (module-count weighted; byte-weighted the same three subjects give 43.0/47.9/49.3, so the count figure is the conservative one and, unlike the count reading, the byte reading rises monotonically with diff breadth). What was RETRACTED, by execution: (a) that `load` is dominated by `referenced_module_paths_in_text`, an unmemoized per-(entry,module) content scan — measured at 4-23ms, ~0% of `load`; (b) that `load` is the dominant cost generally — NOT established either way: it is 67.7-68.1% on a 159-module closure, but on two runs of the SAME 504-module closure it is 38.93% then 49.52%, with typecheck_compute leading the first and `load` the second, so the share ordering flips between repeats of one entry and no share in the partition is a reproducible quantity on this host; (c) that the membership duplication factor multiplies the dominant cost, and the per-source content-hash memo that followed from it. What `load` actually is, measured: ~100% `build_both_closure_edge_index`, a CORPUS-wide edge index that is memoized per `MultiEntryIndex` and costs the same whatever the entry's closure size (51.24s on a 159-module closure vs 50.98s on a 504-module one) though its magnitude is host-noise-dominated (44.96-77.11s across four runs at 2 spans each), so size-independence is the durable claim and the per-index figure is not — so it is fixed per index, not per entry and not per membership. The single-entry `claim_batch` harness pays it twice only because two indices exist on that path (its own, plus `process_shared_index` for machinery). THE BOUND ON ALL OF IT: those shares therefore characterize a fixed-cost-dominated harness, NOT the floor, where one index amortizes across hundreds of entries. The amortization was then MEASURED rather than assumed: on the explicit-entry path (N entries against one shared index), going from 2 spans to 7 grows `load` 1.08x while typecheck_compute grows 2.86x, parse 3.21x and resolve_modules 4.40x, collapsing `load`'s share 67.79% -> 44.97% with no code change — so `load` is paid once per INDEX, not once per entry, and the existing per-index memo already amortizes it. THE REDIRECTION THAT FOLLOWS: a union graph cannot displace a cost that is already paid once, so the program's apparent target is eliminated; the only measured row that scales with BOTH entry count and closure size is typecheck_compute, whose unit of work is module membership — the quantity B measures repeating 38x by count and 48x byte-weighted. Slice 2 CLOSED that candidate as a no-go: three disjoint N<=50 windows and the post-merge representative/reorder receipt found zero repeated typecheck misses and an order-stable distinct-computation set inside one shared typed-cache authority. The durable law remains once-per-content-key; the one-shot ratio instrument was deleted after the merged-SHA provenance receipt. No union construction and no larger-host confirmation run follow from this hypothesis. The separate #7597 successor measures assembly on a new denominator: its exclusive, refusal-based split reproduces symbol-index merge, root symbol-index composition, import-string rewiring, closure symbol-index construction, root variant-base construction, emit-info, pool fill, and services as the priced terms, with the derived other row at 0.32% in both runs. Receipts: [slice 2 closeout](docs/plans/entry-graph-union-slice2-typecheck-attribution.md) and [per-entry assembly decomposition](docs/plans/per-entry-assembly-decomposition-measurement.md). M2: RunnableCompile node gated on v2.std.determinism #5941): [design sketch](docs/plans/floor-shared-compute-memoization.md)"),
      li(text: concat("**five-minute CI gate — central CI program boundary (operator dispatch K, 2026-08-01; sequence revision eager-boar-610, 2026-08-01; contract re-anchored by operator ruling 2026-08-02).** Product contract: every required operation performs the bare minimum computation needed to serve it — no recomputation of any semantic fact whose inputs did not change; wall-clock falls out of the realization/materialization goals rather than defining them. Five minutes is NOT the contract (operator, 2026-08-02: the earlier contract sentence presenting it as such was a mistake) — it is the distress ceiling, 'this is hard / not working well', retained only as the Stage C checkpoint bound; reaching 5:00 does not complete the program while redundant computation remains on a required path. Sub-lane scope and dispatch order are **not restated here**; their single authority is ",
        documentary_ref_symbol_text(reference: five_minute_ci_gate_authority_ref),
        ". This paragraph previously carried its own numbered six-step copy, which went on presenting a superseded order after the roadmap authority was recut — the dual representation §3 names. The citation deliberately names the symbol and asserts nothing about its contents, so a later recut of that note cannot falsify this sentence: summarizing an authority is how a citation becomes a second copy of it. **Native-at-small-scale evidence has landed; operational migration rate remains open.** Which change landed it, at which merge commit, at which evaluated head, and under which executing witness are *execution evidence*, carried in one typed receipt (`gunbc.native_witness_transition_receipt` `native_at_small_scale_transition_receipt`) that this document cites rather than restates — a design criterion embedding a SHA or a pass count has to be edited every time the world moves, and a second hand-maintained copy of one advancing status goes stale silently (operator ruling on #7710, 2026-08-02). Timing ratio remains claimed-not-verified per the design note. **Staged expectations** (not flat wall-clock): Stage A modest admission saving only; Stage B first order-of-magnitude witness reduction after production native cutover; Stage C five-minute gate when all conjunction holds (native majority, materialization hits, no redundant parse/typecheck, pre-index lookup, shared substrate, counted fallback→0). Do not raise CI step cap first — cap lives in `gunbc.ci_spec`. → [five-minute CI gate design](docs/plans/five-minute-ci-gate-design.md) · [witness realization plan](docs/plans/witness-realization-plan.md) · `dag/gunbc/plans/cli_run_hollowing_plan.dag`")),
      li(text: "**#6985 witness-discovery cascade — diagnosis-complete, two confirmed failure classes, both block further stripping.** The initial `pullable()`-never-pulls-arity-zero read was refuted by execution on the `parallelism.dag` restore (DESIGN §5). Two real classes were then confirmed by execution: **Class A** — a re-export-through-partial-strip (qualified-import/re-export mismatch), closed by import-from-definer wave ordering or PR-4 (namespace-resolution-design.md §8). **Class B** — a stripped file's own bare cross-module references resolve only by **pool-membership coincidence**: `resolve_in` finds the target module exactly when some *unrelated* unstripped import elsewhere in the currently-assembled closure has already dragged it into the pool, never from the bare-reference closure itself binding it. A reconciliation probe on an already-stripped `dag/extdeps` file (`bmc/types.dag`) reproduced the identical failure under a narrower closure, confirming this is corpus-wide accidental coverage, not a per-file property — batch-1's ~74 files are green today only because enough unrelated files elsewhere still happen to import the same targets. **This blocks all further `dag/**` import-stripping** until a closure-independent binding fix or a provable-coverage construction check lands. Named side findings, not yet fixed: a LOUDNESS gap (an opaque unlocatable diagnostic for some but not all Class B failure shapes) and an untested zero-arity fn/data-by-value claim. → [import-strip witness-discovery cascade diagnosis](docs/plans/import-strip-witness-discovery-cascade-diagnosis.md)"),
      li(text: "**v1 whole-corpus run stability — the floor's failure axis is retention, not footprint.** The adaptive governor packs width to the memory envelope by construction, so the honest metric is completion-within-step-budget at width > 1; the root cause was a §6 cost-shape defect in typecheck-env construction (multiplicative inductive-field duplication along the import DAG), not corpus size. M1 construction fixes landed fingerprint-identical (#6528). M2's schedule-derived per-module retention landed in #7129 (bd5afd6bc3e8f3e4bc9ded40bcf3b79cdfee536d) and proved the memory thesis at width 1 (9.24 GB executor RSS / 10.7 GB cgroup peak, zero swap, throttle, or pressure); it did not deliver the lane's width > 1 exit bar. Remaining work is the plural-worker outer ring, exact resident-accounting split/reservations, and the width > 1 fleet receipts; v2-eval-as-realization remains the terminal M3 exit. Decision tree, receipts, non-goals: [v1 run-stability throughline](docs/plans/v1-run-stability-throughline.md)"),
      li(text: "**disk-tier repeat-resolve memory growth — CLOSED: OOM fixed in #7728 (b12027cd94f); warm-hit skip-counter proof landed (found 2026-08-02, session quick-swift-501; OOM fixed 2026-08-03; skip proof landed 2026-08-05, session crisp-tern-155).** Historical invalid state (now closed): a second real resolve through the v1 disk-tier cache seam (`v1_compiler.cli_run` `resolved_graph_from_sources_with_index`) against a fresh `MultiEntryIndex` for the same subject exhibited unbounded memory growth (>8GB RSS from ~175MB; `ulimit -v 6000000`, exit 134). That defect reproduced on the already-merged sibling `same_subject_resolves_share_one_graph_store_hits_v2_disk` and was root-caused/fixed in #7728 (store-side bootstrap suppression + re-entrancy refusal); safe controls `repeat_resolve_through_disk_seam_does_not_reenter_provider` and `reentrant_provider_ctx_construction_refuses` are enrolled. The skip-*counter* proof named at discovery time as the remaining gap — no executing witness asserted `TYPECHECK_COMPUTE_COUNT` stayed zero on a warm Hit — WAS landed and is now WITHDRAWN (2026-08-11, gunbc#8146). It read: `cross_process_hit_skips_semantic_recompute` (`v1_compiler_tests.resolve_cross_process_cache_test`) reuses the same once-per-node counter `union_resolve_receipts_test.rs` already uses for the in-process tier, asserting a cold resolve genuinely computes (count > 0) and a fresh-index resolve of the same subject through the disk-tier seam computes nothing (count == 0), run under the exclusive `with_typecheck_compute_count_receipt` wrapper (serial with respect to other counter-reading tests). **That proof no longer executes.** Its module (`resolve_cross_process_cache_test.rs`, and the `union_resolve_receipts_test.rs` it borrowed the counter from) was deleted by the gunbc#8146 v1 Rust test-suite cutover. It was never a live receipt in the first place: the suite was gated by `cargo test --no-run`, so the *green by execution* this paragraph claimed had not been observed since that gate landed — and when the cutover finally ran the module it reported 53 passed / 3 FAILED in 1321s, over the gate's 15-minute budget. The carriers now say so: the `warm-hit-skips-semantic-recompute` clause in `gunbc.materialization_kernel_closing_frontier_audit` reads `NotDelivered`, and `gunbc.ci_cost_arc_closeout_receipt` `warm_hit_settle` = `SkipProofWithdrawn`. RESTORATION: fix the failures, bring the module under budget, re-declare it in `test.retirement.model` — or re-establish the counter assertion as a floor witness. Routing note: the historical memory-envelope class lived in the same v1 floor machinery as the **v1 whole-corpus run stability** lane; that lane's own remaining work (plural-worker outer ring, width > 1 fleet receipts) is unaffected by this closeout."),
      li(text: "**emitted crate partition** — derive the Rust crate layout from the module graph (builds on #6677 derived registration; dissolves the hand-drawn 2-crate split in `stage0_crates.dag`). Shared `CompilationUnit` home with the language-consolidation lane; Rust workflow realization here. → [emitted crate partition design](docs/plans/emitted-crate-partition-design.md)"),
      li(text: "**duplicate work = the content-hash qualification of `Materialization`** — the operator's \"kill compile→…→recompile\" generalized codebase-wide, corrected off a §3 catch: it is NOT a new lens but the qualification step *inside* the spine's `materialize` — the generalization of `std.realization` `reconcile`/`has_collapsible_peer` from the effect-pairwise `create_double_init_collapsible` key to a `ComputationIdentity` lattice (`StructurallyIdentical | NormalizedIdentical | ExtensionallyIdentical\{bound\} | IdentityUnknown\{cause\}`, mirroring `DescentEvidence`). Two coupled halves (assembly line): **Half A** = materialization describes the result of *all* computation (spine lane, `materialize`/FLAG D — not just `RealizedStep` effects); **Half B** = the generalized qualifier + multiplicity-source axis (fork/placement/runtime/scope) that picks the fix (this lane). `.dag` boundedness demotes the \"undecidable\" residue: `IdentityUnknown` is never permanent — it is a *typed, located* `MissingConcept | MissingEnforcement | BoundUnmodeled` debt (the undecidability often *is* a §3 fork), so the lens's product is the located root, not the symptom (§6 moat). Placement-grain flagship (CI release build across 3 `needs:[]` jobs → 1 violation, rewire → clean) proven green-by-execution. No S2 dep; couples to the materialization model. **Kernel closing-contract + delivered-vs-not audit (2026-08-03, doc §11):** the artifact-grain materialization kernel (`dag/std/materialization_provider.dag`, mandate 6B, `#6375`/`#6422`/`#7385`/`#7411`/`#7534`) is contract-stable — four review passes closed every known forgery/omission path on the door — but consumer adoption is 1 of 7 targets wired and that one is opt-in/non-default (`gunbc.materialization_provider_targets` roster); no default CI savings are credited yet, consistent with the five-minute-CI-gate program. → [duplicate-work design](docs/plans/duplicate-work-graph-lens-design.md)"),
      li(text: concat("**body-lowering — make v2's inferred tree faithfully represent runtime iteration.** Stages 1–3 LANDED (#6373): `DescentEvidence` consolidated onto its single authority `dag/std/termination.dag` (§7 ruling, parent 2026-07-03), `loop_multiplicity` is node-aware (termination derived from the `^loop_bound_edge` measure, fail-closed to `DescentUnknown`; FLAG B asymmetry rationale in-code), `04_infer` has a Loop arm typing the iteration fold `τ→τ` with refinement-shaped loops refused, never fabricated (FLAG C), and fold-family calls desugar to seam `Loop` nodes (`fold_lowering.dag`). FLAGs D/E/F operator-SIGNED 2026-07-10 and the within-body FACT layer LANDED (#6443): one sugar rule table (`SugarKey` = surface-atom | production identity, type_alias_rhs migrated as proof), the 3-edge seam Loop (`^loop_carrier_edge`, at-most-one, excluded from the iteration fold in one motion; the lens carrier read is a named-edge lookup, the surface navigation deleted), the lexeme-stamped let binder, and the lens definition closure (`let grown = acc` a PROVEN suspect, transitively; pure-literal RHS decides clean; unbound RHS still refuses — the may_alias false-alarm class is dead; `^fold_accumulator_unread` narrowed to the named-step residue, a Stage-B callee-resolution fact). FLAG D condition: the binding-key re-grounding is REQUIRED cleanup before the feature closes. Remaining keystone: the **general body producer** — real ingested fn bodies still never become substrate behaviors (the MVP `03_body_producer` is fixture-bound to bespoke flat-token productions); the fn_decl→Arrow producer rows + scaffold dissolution remain open, staged behind a typed wrapper-retained frontier (lowered | wrapper-retained\{cause\}, counted, corpus stays green). Its named prerequisite — decl-name binding through the containment index — is no longer restated here; the relevant systems are ",
        documentary_ref_symbol_text(reference: body_lowering_symbol_index_ref),
        ", ",
        documentary_ref_symbol_text(reference: body_lowering_lexical_lookup_ref),
        " and ",
        documentary_ref_symbol_text(reference: body_lowering_production_consumer_ref),
        ", and Stage A remains open. ",
        "Fix = the forward reading of the same `GrammarRelationRow` rows the emitter reads backward (§4 one-grammar-both-directions): one row-selected fold in normalize, exactly-one selection fail-closed, staged within-body → cross-decl → per-op derivation, each stage priced by the refusal buckets it zeroes with RED controls kept live. → [body-lowering design](docs/plans/body-lowering-design.md) · [general body producer design](docs/plans/general-body-producer-design.md)")),
      li(text: "**Rc-ownership wrap-decision — the emitter's §5 fail-open made a construction predicate.** Self-emit for the Rc-heavy core modules (`04_infer`, `06_translate`, `05_emit*` — 100+ `Rc<` sites each in the seed) is blocked by a latent fail-open that silently wraps every `shared_types` member in `Rc<T>`; the fix is ONE structural wrap-decision predicate (derived from ownership facts, never a string-scan of `Rc::new(`) so the bad wrap is unwritable. Gates the deep-emitter self-host lane (Weak → Strong Self Host). → [Rc-ownership wrap-decision design](docs/plans/rc-ownership-wrap-decision-design.md)"),
      li(text: "**witness realization — the v2 execution path.** Emit witnesses to native code (Rust now; C a row-policy option, §7) and use realization/materialization to schedule the runs; the dependency-ordered work that discharges the v2 memory-control findings, none of it gated on self-hosting. → [witness realization plan](docs/plans/witness-realization-plan.md) (findings it discharges: [v2 memory-control audit](docs/plans/v2-memory-control-audit.md))"),
      li(text: "**machine-shape orthogonal scheduling — one realization kernel over faithfully modeled machine facts, with locality (§2).** DRAFT for review; model-before-implement, each phase names its trigger. Its authority transfers to the std/extdeps carriers it names when Phase 1 lands, at which point this md dissolves into a registered `gunbc.plan.Plan` row (§6). → [machine-shape scheduling design](docs/plans/machine-shape-orthogonal-scheduling.md)"),
      li(text: "**namespace-only name resolution — the containment tree is the single naming authority (operator-signed 2026-07-06).** Supersedes resolver-graph-major §1c's *import-name-universe* (\"own declarations ∪ direct import lists\" visibility), carrying forward its mechanics unchanged (binding-edge owner, unbound/double-bound = error, patterns-via-scrutinee, no expected-type picker). One structure — **syntactic containment** — induces everything at once: qualified name = nesting position (`T\{a\}` → `a` *is* `T.a`; `T\{a\{a\}\}` → inner *is* `T.a.a`, so Rule-2 no-ambiguity holds by construction), reference = lexical lookup up the ancestor chain (a sibling module is visible, its members projected `node.Symbol`), `.` = projection one level down (field / module-member / variant — one op), and `.` *is* the `std.induction` sub-value relation (descent evidence). So one content-addressed tree grounds THREE consumers — resolution *walks* it, content-addressing *hashes* it, termination reads its *sub-value* edges (§2 Realization: one structure, N consumers). Frontend BUILDS the tree from nesting (pure structure, zero resolution logic); backend WALKS it (lexical up, project down) — no heuristics to tune (the tuning risk = resolving against a flat index instead of the tree). Two governing rules (operator, 2026-07-06): **no dual representation / minimal at every layer; no ambiguity at any layer.** Rides on `SymbolIndex` = \"the containment tree materialized\" (`type-env-single-authority`, lively-raven lane) — the loyal-heron scaling gate that formerly blocked resolver surgery was **discharged at #6809** (per §7.5 of the design doc: \"the lane is no longer blocked on it\"); `import-scoped` (§1c, today) and `namespace-only-Y` (position-info uniqueness: expected type filters a variant to one, never picks) are two walk-rules over one *fill-agnostic* index (fill = whole tree; policy gates lookup, never fill). Census: 98% of names globally unique → always bare; the residue is v1-seed forks (resolve by subtree, free) + github-style variants ((Y) context-resolves); no consolidate-now forks (the census's 2 same-name flags proved to be a homonym + whole-file v1-seed duplication, not genuine cross-tree §3 forks). Terminal step: delete the `import` grammar + supporting code → `import` becomes a *parse error*, deps derived from `container.member` references (Rule-1 end-state). → [namespace-only resolution design](docs/plans/namespace-resolution-design.md)"),
      li(text: "**fleet-reconcile spine — one grain-agnostic membership diff (deploy · fleet · session).** ONE `membership_reconcile<M,K>` reuses `std.change.keyed_two_way_diff` verbatim (no fork): desired set vs observed set keyed by member IDENTITY — Added → `MemberAdded\{to}`, Modified → `MemberChanged\{from,to}`, Removed → `MemberRemoved`-or-`MemberRemovalRefused`, Unchanged → noop (absence of a hunk). **Add and change are separate arms, not one `upsert`** (operator ruling): the collapse discarded `from`, which is precisely the fact a safe realization of a change needs — an SCM ref update is a compare-and-swap naming the expected prior (`git update-ref <ref> <new> <old>`), and a host resource whose address moved needs the prior endpoint to retire, so with only `to` in hand the sole reachable realization is the unconditional write. `gunbc.change_realization` `classify_change` is the table that consumes both sides: SCM ref → atomic CAS; source declaration move → atomic SCM tree transformation; same runtime address → in-place update; changed host address → staged replacement with **declared** intermediates. That last row is the srv4 lesson made unwritable rather than warned about — `StagedIntermediates` is head+tail so an empty intermediate plan has no constructor, and an undeclared one REFUSES (typed, located) instead of degrading to an overwrite. It also kills a live conflation: a `.dag` module re-home is an SCM tree transformation, **not** a host migration. **Only removal consults ownership**, and that asymmetry is deliberate: `gunbc.ownership` defines `Ensured` as 'requires present but does NOT own (never torn down)', a constraint on removal, so converging a drifted member the model does not own is the intended behavior of an `Ensured` member rather than a violation of it (`tool_readiness` exists to re-install a drifted pin). A change-refusal arm was built during this work and WITHDRAWN for over-firing on exactly that; the regression control `w_ensured_member_converges_but_refuses_removal` keeps it withdrawn by execution rather than by prose. A member may be a service/unit/host OR a Session (operator: 'it should all be the same'), so a new member type is a new instantiation of the same fn with its own `(key_of, key_eq, value_eq, ownership_of)` bundle — zero spine change (a Realization, §2; a forked reconcile means the genericity bought nothing). R5 removal-owned-only is a CONSTRUCTION WALL: a Removed non-owned member yields `MemberRemovalRefused`, which has no effect arm in any apply dispatch, so the bad state is unwritable — never a post-check, never 'assume owned' (the absorbing fallback §5 forbids); ownership-unknown REFUSES too, typed/located/counted. Ownership is extracted as its own single authority (`gunbc.ownership.Ownership`) and `live_deploy`'s `DeploymentStep` re-grounds onto it (pass 2, a declared dissolution trigger — never a second `Owned|Ensured`). Pass 1 (spine + R5 + a synthetic discriminating witness the degenerate apply/retract poles cannot exercise) landed. Identity is **declaration-owned and allocator-minted**, never derived from unit/path/content (operator ruling): `gunbc.host_resource` carries `HostResourceIdentity\{host, occurrence}` and `HostResource<R, V>\{identity, role, value}`, where the occurrence is minted by an allocator, `role` is a **type parameter** (so a new role is an instantiation, never a widened central enum), and unit name / FIFO / storage path / configuration are mutable *value* fields. That is what makes a rename ONE `MemberChanged` rather than a delete-plus-create some key function must be clever enough to re-pair — git does not track renames, it INFERS them from content similarity, and §4 rules a heuristic never necessary in a closed system; a content-or-path-derived `key_of` is that same inference wearing a bundle. It is a carrier distinct from `std.occurrence_identity` deliberately: that module is specifically about SOURCE occurrences (its `OccurrenceCategory` enumerates callable/type/field/method, its allocator is coupled to `AuthoredTokenOrdinalSpace`), and sharing one `Int` space across two subjects would let `occurrence_id_eq` answer `true` on a numeric coincidence — the cross-family comparison the `ContentHash` family grounding closed. Extracting one subject-parameterized minted-identity carrier (the `Vendor<Domain>` shape) is registered as a trigger, not built under a load-bearing module with stage0 work in flight. The `(key_of, key_eq, value_eq, ownership_of)` bundle each instantiation supplies is the visible end of a deeper gap: `std` models keyed *collections* — `std.keyed_row`, `std.keyed_roster`, `std.change` `keyed_two_way_diff` — but does not model *keying* itself, so every consumer re-supplies the relation ad hoc and several defects already fixed one lane at a time share that one unnamed cause. Design note only, no code lands from it yet. → [membership-diff reconcile spine design](docs/plans/membership-diff-reconcile-spine-design.md) · [keying as a modelled relation](docs/plans/keying-relation-design.md)"),
      li(text: "**one authorization kernel, several typed request profiles (operator directive 2026-08-01).** `std.access.AccessRequest` carries subject, action, object, typed context, and typed evidence; `AccessPolicy` is the sole policy-to-decision seam, `decision_meet` the sole conjunction, and every denial carries a typed cause at structured request coordinates. Effect, reference, publication admission, disclosure, and sealed execution are exact request products projected into that kernel, never independent allow/deny folds. Principals project external evidence — OIDC issuer/subject and observed POSIX effective principal — rather than being invented internal identities. The provisional gunbc#7541 `Verb::Reference` / `Verb::Publish` / `AudienceScopeTree` carriers dissolved: audiences are sets with join and partial-order subset, because real audiences can overlap without either containing the other. `PublicationAdmissionRequest` is only the generic request shape here: the operator-deleted Stage-0 placement wall, publisher policy, and pathname census are deliberately absent."),
      li(text: "**publication and reference-admission capabilities — carrier retirement, replacement ontology unresolved.** **Removal record (operator ruling 2026-08-01):** the Stage-0 `Publish`/`Reference` effect-grant experiment retired after its sole live publication consumer, the placement gate, was deleted. `Reference` and `Publish` were removed from `std.effect_grant.Verb`, `AudienceScopeTree` was removed, and `std.visibility_grant` plus its carrier-only witnesses were deleted; generic authorization admission re-homed onto the `std.access` kernel. This retires those carriers, not the underlying capabilities. Publication remains required, but no replacement ontology has been selected and any redesign requires operator review before implementation. Reference-edge admission — whether an edge may form within one compiled program — is a separate deferred capability awaiting a concrete consumer; the publication rollback decides nothing about it. **Stage 0 per-file placement gate DELETED:** its constant `Publish World` rows duplicated Git's post-cutover path population, four same-day repair rounds raced identical reconstructions and repeatedly redded fleet CI, ordinary modifications were invisible, and a CI check after a public push could protect merge integrity but not confidentiality because the objects had already reached public storage. New public files require no stamp. Any future confidentiality boundary must act before unauthorized bytes enter public storage — for example by withholding public-write capability from ordinary sessions — but that constraint selects no replacement architecture. The locked extension remains a **publication capability product, not a ladder**: source/interface disclosure, artifact delivery, local/remote execution, integrity, reproducibility, existence/churn disclosure, revocation/erase, and placement/trust are independent policy axes. An emitted artifact is not source disclosure but remains inspectable; client-held key means plaintext access, distinct from remote/attested key custody. Locked blobs use AEAD with subject/interface/version/audience/policy context bound as associated data plus confidentiality-and-integrity-preserving per-audience key wraps; wrong key/context/authentication is typed refusal. Per-statement holes remain refused; interface/realization is the cut; size padding and `Secret`/`Pii`→`SecretRef` construction constraints remain. Commitments never promise zero leakage: length, timing, interface shape, wrap count, access pattern, low-entropy guessing, and churn stay in a named threat model. Remote execution and SCM diagnostics obey information-flow/noninterference + explicit declassification; metering prices an oracle but does not secure it, and unauthorized audiences receive only a non-leaking diagnostic projection. Cryptographic erase is conditional on every key/wrap/backup/cache/derivative/recovery path being sanitized and the result validated, never “delete one key.” → [retired node/subtree carrier experiment](docs/plans/node-subtree-visibility-grants.md)"),
      li(text: "**effect grants over namespaces — dissolve `Hermetic | Wet` into (frame × verb × subtree).** The 2-valued `Hermetic | Wet` enum conflates four axes (input closure / replayability, output reach / interference, handler binding, selection eligibility) — a §3 state-space conflation with proto-envelopes already forked (the hand-rolled `workspace_root` containment gate, `std.resources.ResourceHandle`, `AuthScope`, `LiveTreeDisposition`). End shape (§3, cited not minted): an effect target is a position in a containment tree that already exists — filesystem paths, URIs, `/proc`, service-operation paths, and the unifying case **code names themselves** (the containment tree the namespace-resolution lane makes the single naming authority); permission is a grant of (verb × subtree) attached to a `Frame`; admissibility is the namespace prefix relation — the same `⊑` the resolver walks, content-addressing hashes, and termination reads, so effects become the **fourth consumer** of the one containment structure. Dispatch projects an `EffectRequest` through `std.access.AccessPolicy` fail-closed (`Deny` carries the typed/located/counted refusal, never a silent widen — §5); *replayable* / *isolated* / `LiveTreeDisposition` become **derived** projections, and `Hermetic | Wet | Record` survive only as named envelope presets, deleted at the end. Frame-containment is graded on the §5 construction/validation axis (`LifecycleByConstruction` vs `LifecycleByConvention`). No code lands from the design doc; the FLAG-A interim is the only near-term consumer. → [effect-namespace-grants design](docs/plans/effect-namespace-grants.md)"),
      li(text: "**shell → intent: the intent layer is language-blind (operator-aligned 2026-07-17; census/routing consolidated onto sibling authorities 2026-07-19).** §3/§4 restated for the shell case: the `.dag` intent may not name a target language — a workflow is an ordinary dependency graph over modeled operations (§4: a program is `Node`+`Edge`); *the intent IS the `.dag` graph* (operator), and `src/v2/std/orchestration.dag` `Pipeline` is optional sugar, never the authority. Two distinct downstream layers, not to be conflated: **emit** (§4 — `emit(intent, Bash)`, one grammar read backward, target as a *parameter*) and **realization** (§2 — pure-spec → host-effect, N transports `LocalShell`/`SshShell`, `dag/gunbc/host_effect_realize.dag`). **Routing (the load-bearing correction, operator review 2026-07-19): most sites do NOT emit bash.** Runtime-present sites (a gunbc runtime / typed transport is on the path) realize as typed service ops / typed transports / binary-interpreted effect-plan via `host_effect_apply` — no bash emitted (precedent already in tree: `floor_diff_observe` calls `git.Core.DiffUnified0` directly; `host_identity_converge` funnels through `host_effect_apply`); only foreign executors (GHA `run:`, cron, git hooks) and pre-runtime bootstrap windows render through the bounded v2 bash grammar rows. The authority for that routing is `authorize_shell_emission` (provisioning-window lane); the per-site residual census + arc-completion scoping is `shell-to-dag-residual-census-and-arc-completion.md` (witty-ibex-317) — DESIGN carries no parallel copy of either. **Invariant (enforceable):** the intent imports no language-construction vocab — emit-internal; `src/v2/lens/realization_vocabulary_containment.dag` confines it (LANDED #6854). The lens confines by MODULE, and naming what it actually reads is the whole point of §3's cite-the-symbol rule: `target_ast_vocab_module_prefixes` = `v2.extdeps.languages.bash`, `v2.workflow.bash`, plus `target_ast_vocab_modules` = `extdeps.languages.typescript.program`. The bash vocabulary is `v2.extdeps.languages.bash_build`'s `bash_build_*` constructors and the `v2.workflow.bash_command_fold_serialize` backend. *(Corrected 2026-07-31: this sentence previously listed the confined vocab as ``bash_build``, ``bash_command_fold_serialize``, ``the ShellStmt/ShellWord/ShellProgram coproduct``, ``serialize_bash``. The first two are real modules; the last two NAME NOTHING — the bash AST is built as generic `Node` (every `bash_build_*` fn returns `Node`), there is no ShellStmt/ShellWord/ShellProgram coproduct, and no `serialize_bash` is defined anywhere in the tree. Both were dissolved by the typed-WitnessBin migration (Phase D, 2026-07-14), which the `dag/tools/*` transport notes record and this row did not follow. The WALL WAS NEVER AFFECTED — the lens confines module prefixes, not these names, so nothing was unguarded; what rotted was the description of it, in the sentence declaring the invariant. That is the §3 positional/symbolic citation class exactly: a name-level claim in the canonical authority that a grep would have decided, sitting false while the mechanism it describes stayed correct. Found by a corpus citation sweep, not by anyone touching this row.)* Meta-exec confinement is **PARTIAL**: Wave A1 (#6900) module-isolated `shell.Exec.Run/.Check` and the import wall now has an empty exception roster, and the construction half is CLOSED at the type: `TransportScript` has exactly one mint, sealed by `admit_callers` to two named production decls, so no module can form a transport value by literal or by cast. What is still open is one rung lower and named precisely: the two admitted bridges take a bare `String`, so any caller of `gunbc.retained_shell_script` `retained_transport` / `retained_runtime` can still route arbitrary text into a transport — counted, reason-bearing and dissolve-on-carrying at every call, but a conspicuous route rather than an impossible one. That residue dissolves with Phase 2, when the runtime-present sites become typed effects on `host_effect_apply` and stop needing a String bridge at all. *(Corrected 2026-07-26: this read \"`host_language_transport_script` lens inert, `fail_closed_lockdown.dag`\". That lens is **LIVE** — activated by #7184 as a per-PR `ReadsLiveTree` consumer that reds a new raw literal at an enrolled `Run` position — and the transport-script hole it was cited for is **closed by construction on the `ShellOnHost` route only**: `ShellOnHost.script` is the `RetainedShellScript` record and the free minter is deleted — and the `shell.Exec.Run` half is now closed the same way: `TransportScript` is a `sole_constructor` RECORD, not a transparent brand, so neither a cross-module record literal nor an `as TransportScript` cast can produce one, and `extdeps.shell.exec` `transport_script_seal` is the single mint, `admit_callers`-sealed to exactly two production decls (`gunbc.retained_shell_script` `retained_shell_script_to_transport` and `gunbc.bash_materialized_transport` `bash_materialized_transport`) with no test declaration admitted. The cast half required extending the language, not only the carrier: `sole_constructor` was enforced at the record literal only, and `validate_cast` judges a cast only when source AND target both sit in `std.coercion` `dag_cast_rules`, so a cast into a sealed type was unjudged — `04_infer` `sole_constructor_construction_diags` is now the one authority both construction forms consult (corpus census at landing: zero `as <sealed type>` casts existed across all 17 sole_constructor types, so the form closed without refusing any existing site). What the record cost is a transport projection, proven by execution rather than assumed: `stdin: script.body` delivers exact bytes and `argv` interpolates the same record field into the `sh -c` slot. The lens stays deliberately green on `ComputedApplication`, so it backstops literal blobs rather than being the wall. What remains open is the String-bridge residue named above, not the construction wall and not the already-landed module-import wall.)* **Carriers → `Do\{effect\}` (never a command string, host-effect-orchestration.md):** the anemic `String` leaves are `host_effect.ShellCommand\{script\}`, `std.orchestration.Run.command`, and `host_effect.BootstrapFragment.script` — add-replacement → migrate all consumers → delete at arc close (§5 construction; `host_effect.dag` is load-bearing, the delete escalated). **Phases:** 0 sidecar delete LANDED (#6831) → 1 agnostic `While`/`Retry` emit PARTIAL (#6832; corrected 2026-07-26, verified against main — `While` framing has exactly one production consumer, `ci_floor_peak_emit`, whose cond and body are still raw shell strings; `BoundedPoll` had zero production construction sites and was deleted 2026-08-07 (Node-tree constructor census over production roots, planted-construction/match-pattern/string-literal controls); #7303 makes Retry refuse multi-step bodies, present redirect/capture, changed template log sources, and three-or-more (N-level) escalation shapes; #7318 additionally refuses every nonempty source `Run.env` while preserving empty-env emission bytes, proven by executing nonempty-refusal and empty-env accepted controls. THE FIRST OF PHASE 1'S THREE RESIDUES IS CLOSED (2026-08-05): the live `While` consumer's two raw while-loop command leaves are gone — `ci_floor_peak_emit` carries one `std.path_intent` `NearestAncestorContaining` row `ci_floor_peak_nearest_ancestor_spec`, and `ci_floor_peak_while_cond_command` / `ci_floor_peak_while_body_command` derive through `bash_orch_if` `bash_nearest_ancestor_locate_cond_command` / `bash_nearest_ancestor_locate_body_command` (the same `bash_path_predicate_target_model` / `bash_dirname_ascent_target_model` lowering `v2.workflow.orchestration_while_emit`'s hand-authored goldens pin, now reached via the bundled spec rather than separate `PathPredicate` / `DirnameAscent` leaves). The refusal arm carries a located poison rather than empty text, because the `Run.command` seam is `String`-typed and cannot carry an `Outcome`. Two facts keep this from reading as more than it is. The seam itself did NOT climb — derived text still flows into an untyped field, and the dissolve-on is `Predicate` / `PipelineStep` gaining path arms so `orch_emit_step` lowers at emission time (`ci_floor_peak_path_leaf_residue_note`). THE THIRD LEAF CLOSED WITH THE SAME VERTICAL (#7978, 2026-08-07): `ci_floor_peak_assign_cgroup_dir_command` is no longer a raw procfs one-liner — it composes `bash_nearest_ancestor_locate_assign_command` over `ci_floor_peak_nearest_ancestor_spec`, which lowers unified-hierarchy membership through `extdeps.linux.proc_self_cgroup` + `v2.extdeps.languages.bash_proc_self_cgroup` with procfs path `extdeps.linux.procfs` `ProcSelfCgroup`, fail-closed on missing/ambiguous/malformed membership and cross-realization witnessed. Phase 2 residue on this pipeline is the foreign-executor runtime-scan transport (`bash_membership_assign_from_source`) and peak reset/cat/echo calibration leaves (typed filesystem observation), not the membership decode. Landing the While residue also transposed `[ ! -f X ]` to `! [ -f X ]` at four generated-workflow sites and three goldens — a POSIX equivalence proven by execution across `sh`/`dash`/`bash` on eight operand classes and four synthetic cgroup-tree shapes, not by reading the spec. Phase 1 remains PARTIAL for the foreign-executor/bootstrap residue (`BoundedPoll` closed by deletion, 2026-08-07) → 2 model runtime-present sites as typed effects on `host_effect_apply` + emit the foreign-executor/bootstrap residue → 3 wall green (bash-AST containment + meta-exec confinement). → [shell → intent design](docs/plans/shell-intent-emit-realization-design.md)"),
      li(text: "**language and medium support completion — profiles, registry, exact frontier (program authority; LANG-0 identity/registry LANDED #7803, TS-0 no-new-debt gate LANDED 2026-08-06).** Every claimed target is complete for an explicit support profile or visibly partial/parked — never mostly-supported. **TS-0 receipt (executed 2026-08-06):** five exact leak edges at `(importer_path, vocab_module)` grain frozen on `v2.lens.realization_vocabulary_containment` `realization_vocab_grandfathered_edge_roster` (two product-layer TS-AST debts, three misplaced realization edges; no sanctioned boundaries in the current population); `realization_vocab_live_corpus_receipt_holds` PASS on current main and enrolled on falsifier substrate long lane — **that enrollment carries no executions**: the cadence produced no green verdict after 2026-08-03 and the floor cut deleted it outright on 2026-08-15, so the row is enrolled against a lane that does not run (see the CI rung drop below; enrollment is not coverage); new exact edges refuse via `no_new_debt_gate_test.dag`. Remaining program: LANG-1 exact residue census · LANG-3 common execution receipt · LANG-4 derived status projection · TS-1..TS-8 migration (no breadth in TS-0). Three operator decisions still open: ecmascript authority, cpp profile, TsProgram terminal role. → [language-support-completion design](docs/plans/language-support-completion-design.md)"),
      li(text: "**srvN build-cache provisioning on host-standup subsumption spine.** Legacy `ctrl-sccache.service` is misconfigured; `ci_release_build_script()` absorbing fallback (`CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=1` then `-u RUSTC_WRAPPER`) masks the deficit (§5). Design anchor: provision sccache as a host-effect ensure on the assimilation spine (P1b after `RunnerDeploySlot`), generalizing srv3's `WorkflowEnsureActuatorToolchain` to srvN; `ProvisionBuildCache { catalog_id }` routes through `host_effect_apply`; verdict fold is `ProvisionConverged | ProvisionRefused` only. **PLACEMENT IS A VERDICT AXIS (landed):** sccache executes cache-miss compiles as *children of the server*, so the server's cgroup is the cgroup every compile on the host runs in — a responsive daemon proves nothing about where it runs, and collapsing the two into one `DaemonStatsOk` arm is the §5 state-space conflation that let a lazily auto-spawned server (kept alive by `SCCACHE_IDLE_TIMEOUT=0`) strand itself in a dead slot's cgroup and bound all five live slots' compiles at one slot's cap. `BuildCacheServerPlacement = ServerManagedUnitOwned | ServerRunnerOwned\{slot,lifecycle\} | ServerUnsupervised | ServerPlacementAbsent | ServerPlacementUnknown` gates `ProvisionConverged`; unknown placement refuses rather than assuming owned, and BOTH runner lifecycles refuse (an active slot means birth ordering failed; a retired slot means birth ordering AND retirement both failed). It is derived from an endpoint-owner observation homed with the deployed-instance model in `gunbc.build_cache_instance` (`BuildCacheInstance` = host x consumer class x instance name, distinct from the CATALOG identity `sccache_local_id`), because process name plus cgroup cannot tell a server from the transient clients every compile spawns — the owner is the process holding the LISTEN on the configured endpoint. **LIVE RECEIPT 2026-08-01 (read-only, srv1/srv2/srv3):** NO host has a unit-owned CI cache; the birth defect is fleet-wide. srv1's owner is pid 3975946, 31.2 days old, in RETIRED slot `srv1-10` (inactive+disabled) with `max 229780` / `oom_kill 12` and a live runner's `rustc` resident beside it; srv2's and srv3's owners sit in ACTIVE slots and so self-limit only because ephemeral runners recycle them. srv4 was unreachable and is carried as typed `ReachRefused`, never as absent. A measured correction landed with it: `--show-stats` does NOT auto-start a server (private-endpoint controls, both UDS and TCP, 0 to 0 listeners) — it answers plausibly with NO server in existence, so stats may only ever be supplemental data read after ownership is grounded; a real COMPILE is what spawns a server, inheriting the invoking cgroup. The budget consequence is a first-class `compile_pool` child of the host allocation tree sized by the cited jobserver authority (`jobserver_build_pool_bytes`) — a per-slot memory cap does *not* bound compile RSS — with `CompilePoolPlacement` making the pool's size derive from the declared topology rather than a fudge. STEP 2 (fallback removal) operator-sequenced after T4 live read-back. → [srvN build-cache provisioning design](docs/plans/srvn-buildcache-provisioning-design.md) · [placement step-1 live receipt](docs/plans/build-cache-placement-receipt.md)"),
      li(text: "**srv4 fleet subsumption — manual-gap receipts (2026-07-22).** srv4 was installed and subsumed through the modeled BMC virtual-media path (PR #7027: 5 runners live, rootless Docker + sccache, seeded-media boot-fidelity fixes root-caused live); every step still performed by hand is typed as a modeling gap in the registered plan `gunbc.plans.fleet_subsumption_manual_gaps` (A1 MaterializedSecret lifecycle, A2 reach-secrets ADC, A3 SecretRef liveness, B1 runner-slot provisioning, B2 runner user, B3 fresh-host prereqs, B4 install actuator), each with an acceptance bar and RED; the plan dissolves item-by-item and fully retires when srv5 subsumes with zero hand-run shell. The BMC credential-rotation gap analysis that seeded the lane: → [srv4 BMC onboarding gap](docs/plans/srv4-bmc-onboarding-gap.md)"),
      li(text: "**secret version lifecycle — a subject-agnostic rotation kernel (SECRET-VERSION-LIFECYCLE-0).** `gunbc.auth.secret_rotation` holds the version-rotation sequence once, parameterized by a `SecretRotationIntent`: observe → obtain replacement → **AddVersion exactly once** → read back the exact new version → verify the payload is the one written → verify the named consumers against that exact version → retire the prior → emit a `SecretRotationReceipt`. The kernel names no subject; its witness uses SYNTHETIC fixtures only. Wire decoding is **imported, never reimplemented** — `decode_sm_access_version_wire_response`, `decode_sm_resolved_version_identity_wire` and `SmResolvedVersionIdentity` with their typed refusals come from `extdeps.cloud.gcp.secret_manager` (landing jointly with #7995) — and **no arm binds a typed refusal to `_`**, because the incident this lane replaces was a production path that did exactly that and returned a fabricated `Secret` while every witness stayed green. The interface half adds `ListVersions`, `GetVersion`, `DisableVersion`, `EnableVersion` and `DestroyVersion` beside `AddVersion`; only disable and enable carry `idempotent`, because only they converge on re-issue — **AddVersion is not idempotent (every call mints a version, so a retry sprawls) and destroy is not (it erases bytes with no inverse)**. Walls, each with an executable forbidden arm: **exactly-once** — retry identity comes from a persisted attempt record plus the resolved identity, never from re-calling AddVersion; the issued-but-unconfirmed state is its own arm, resolved by *observing* the store, and refuses on a truncated inventory because a false *did not add* authorizes a second add. **Evidence must name a version** — success-only through the moving `latest` alias is refused. **Evidence selection classifies missing / unique / ambiguous** rather than taking the first match, since two disagreeing reports for one consumer is a contradiction and first-wins would render it a verdict. **Census completeness is an identity join** over a declared roster of branded `ConsumerId`, not a count. **A retirement is receipt-backed, never assertable**: `RetirementApplied` is reachable only through `DisableReceipt`/`DestroyReceipt`, each admitted only from an operation response whose observed state CONFIRMS the claim, so *we issued the call* cannot report as *it is done*. **Stated property of the kernel: no rotation artifact stores a payload digest, for any subject, at any strength** — a digest of a low-entropy or published string is credential material wearing a hash, and scoping the rule would require classifying entropy the workflow must not inspect, so the receipt keeps a payload-identity *verdict* and no arm can carry bytes. Branch cleanliness and credential revocation are separate standings; retirement-not-attempted is *known* still-live, not unknown. **Frontier, declared not stubbed:** nothing actuates — the operator-token route is refuted upstream (`ACCESS_TOKEN_TYPE_UNSUPPORTED`) and `gunbc.github_actions_wif` is absent from `origin/main` (measured 2026-08-13): the federation rows exist only on `origin/session/pi-reach-0`, whose PR #7971 closed 2026-08-07 without merge — dead work, not in flight. Revival onto a live branch versus fresh authoring remains an operator decision; actuation names (`github_actions_wif_pool`, `github_actions_wif_provider`, `github_actions_wif_provider_resource`, `github_actions_wif_attribute_condition`, `github_actions_wif_principal_set`) and deliberately does not copy them as a private duplicate until that single federation authority exists on main. **Explicitly out of scope, and blocked on observation:** rotating any *live fleet credential*. That is a separate lane (BMC-CREDENTIAL-ROTATE-0) which may not begin until a WIF-backed READ-ONLY probe establishes the target credential state, probing factory and managed access INDEPENDENTLY — both-accepted and neither-accepted are real states with different remedies, so a first-wins probe is a product collapsed into a coproduct. Until such a probe runs, the only honest standing for any BMC account is *unobserved*, and a committed fixture is authored data rather than an observation of it. Witness: `dag/test/claim/secret_rotation_witness_test.dag`."),
      li(text: "**gunb.ai site subsumption — hosting + frontend emitted from `.dag` (operator-directed 2026-07-20).** Subsume `gunb-ai/frontend`: the public site's HTML/CSS/JS become emitted, drift-gated artifacts in this repo, published to GitHub Pages at `gunb.ai` (DNS already points at Pages IPs; cutover is Pages-config-only; the frontend repo archives). Register thesis, developed across nine operator passes and carried as a signable checklist + behavioral-intricacy law, REVISED at the S1 selective-spectacle amendment (operator recalibration 2026-08-02): *calm field; luminous focus; physical response; every response true* — the former *quiet at arm's length* reading correctly rejected uniform spectacle but was read as uniform austerity (the gap ruling's no-protagonist finding), so the amendment keeps every quiet-field law and adds the missing half as three salience principles carried by `gunbc.design.salience` (selective-spectacle: energy granted per SalienceRole from one budget table, at most one ordinary focal subject per region, Critical may interrupt; emission-over-material: a crisp-core/low-alpha-corona emission layer over restrained material, granted only beside a non-emission discriminator, no pulse field exists; bounded-attraction: the visual plate travels inside a fixed hit region on the register's one spring, and renderer-local motion never advances domain state). Simplicity remains a D0/D1 property, intricacy a D2/D3 property of BEHAVIOR (coverage × consistency × timing, the Discord re-read), and every intricate thing shown or answered derives from a real repo fact. Phase-0 slices LANDED under `dag/gunbc/site/` (principles → tokens → interaction → mark → constellation → moodboard → theme_transition → starlight/accent_study), proven by execution with RED controls: themes as total role→material assignments (night = warm starlight on indigo, day = indigo ink on paper, one model, two projections; totality/area/contrast witnessed), the glare envelope (bright OR chromatic, never both — the operator-rejected gamer cyan is the negative control and provably absent from emissions), still-until-touched by construction (MotionTrigger has no ambient variant), the universal theme reframe derived from the same BuildRule rows (role-routed colors cross-fade for free on one token clock; an unthemed color is a counted census row; the data-theme watcher pins the flip so the fade cannot race the UA restyle — measured, snap → 12–16-frame blend), and the accent decision grounded in `extdeps.astronomy.stellar_classification` (cited MK/Harvard table; no green stars exist so green enters only as verdigris material; M self-prunes at the envelope; candidates O/A/G/K rendered from identical fact rows). Remaining: checklist row-by-row sign-off, primitives.html at every depth, mark candidates B–D, craft-floor scales, generated-artifact registration, Pages workflow + cutover receipt (discharges the `2-stateless-frontend` milestone-A shape on the public domain). → [site subsumption design](docs/plans/gunb-ai-site-subsumption-design.md)"),
      li(text: "**cross-platform instrument motion — capability bindings, a synthetic impulse, and a bob that settles (operator-directed 2026-08-04; verdict withdrawn 2026-08-05).** The lane exists because of the mistake that opened it: a browser probe found `DeviceMotionEvent` constructible, never called `start()`, and a verdict of *sensor absent* was asserted from it — refuted three ways, because the specification's hollow-event allowance is a **SHOULD**, constructor presence is not sensor presence, and the probe ran inside an artifact whose Permissions Policy default allowlist is `'self'`, so the decisive question was never asked. Receipt kept, verdict withdrawn, observation reclassified `MotionReachabilityUnresolved`. **Four layers stay separate** because collapsing them is how a browser became the architecture: product semantics, application container, renderer realization and platform capability bindings are independent axes on `gunbc.host_realization` `HostRealization`, a browser is ONE realization, and **OS support is not one Boolean** — each provider is its own extdeps authority (`extdeps.w3c.device_orientation_and_motion`, `extdeps.w3c.generic_sensor`, `extdeps.w3c.accelerometer`, `extdeps.browser.google_chrome` as a distribution distinct from the Chromium engine), so a platform is a row and never a wider product enum. **The support wall took two reviews to become real:** naming `qualify_tap` the sole minter of `TapQualified` did not make it one, because that arm is a public constructor and the stream handed the verdict an already-qualified value — so a caller could author it around a failing receipt and reach `CapabilitySupported`. The stream now carries the RAW requirement and receipt and `derive_capability_verdict` qualifies them itself; the negative control lost the `Passed`/`Failed` arms whose NAME qualification trusted and derives its verdict from the observed idle peak; a measurement clearing thresholds the model calls unsigned lands on `CapabilityQualifiedAgainstProvisionalRequirement`, which is not the key the durable-claim gate reads; and an unmeasured detection latency is `LatencyUnobserved` rather than zero, zero being the BEST possible latency and therefore the worst available answer. **Web admission moved onto the decision path:** `derive_reachability` and `project_binding_stream` matched the stream arm alone, so an insecure context with a blocked policy, a denied permission and arriving readings still derived reachable — a witness proved the checker noticed, nothing proved a contradicted observation had no route to a verdict. Both consumers run the check first, deliberately rather than routing an admitted-observation wrapper, since a substrate without opaque construction would make that wrapper the same forgery one level up; coherence reads the gyroscope policy DeviceMotion requires, treats an unanswered prompt as contradicting readings, and derives required axes per provider rather than demanding a `requestPermission` exposure from sensors that have none. **The bob did not settle, and the reason is worth keeping:** the envelope decayed by each STEP's duration, so at the production sixteen-millisecond frame against a one-hundred-and-eighty-millisecond half-life it computed zero half-lives and fell by nothing, the budget truncated the sequence to an empty tail, and the client set `translateY(0)` on its own — between them a model that never settled rendered as one that had. Amplitude is now a function of CUMULATIVE elapsed time with linear interpolation inside each half-life; descent evidence is MEASURED by comparing two amplitudes and returns `DescentUnknown` on growth; the two-valued oscillation became a triangle over four quarter-period legs because a square wave satisfies 'appears on both sides of rest' while never occupying a position between them; budget exhaustion is `BobSequenceRefused`; the client's manufactured final zero is deleted. **And the keystone was not conjoined** — five frame claims sat in a discarded expression above the returned one, which is how all of this reported green, and the same shape was then found in the web witness: a claim that is called but not joined is indistinguishable from one that is enforced. **A third review found five more seams, and four of them were one shape — a name standing where a fact was owed.** ONE REQUIREMENT: the observation carried two independently writable requirements, one on the binding and one on the tap outcome, so a row could name signed thresholds while measuring against provisional ones; they are now `BoundCapabilityRequirement { identity, snapshot }` and the snapshot is what qualification reads, asserted in both directions by a control that swaps the label and the numbers. NAMES: `PhysicalImpulse.source_binding` pointed at a producer function and is `source_provider`; the coverage state fused *is it written* with *has anything run*, and its `receipt` field carried that same producer reference — it splits into `BindingImplementationState` and `BindingProbeState`, and the one row that claims a probe now cites an executing witness. ONE WINDOW: three web stream arms and two binding stream arms each carried a `window_ms` beside the observation's own `observation_window_ms`, so a record whose stream reported four seconds inside a window of zero was constructible; the arms keep only what is theirs. THE THIRD PATH: `project_sensor_availability` — the projection feeding the sandbox's admission input — was still reading the stream arm alone after the other two consumers were moved onto the coherence check, so the exact bypass that repair closed stayed open where a consumer acts on the answer. TWO ABSORPTIONS, at the page: `frames_for`/`frames_wire` answered a refused sequence with an empty list and the empty string, which made every `string_contains(html, wire)` control VACUOUSLY TRUE — the serializer now takes frames a caller obtained by matching the settled arm, and every wire claim asserts non-emptiness first; and `BobSequenceRefused` reported the REMAINING budget, always zero, under a name a reader takes for the budget allowed, so the field is `budget_allowed` and the control pins three rather than the zero it used to enshrine. AND THE PROBES MISCLASSIFIED READINGS: the event path tested `acceleration.x` alone and the sensor path counted reading EVENTS while testing nothing, so a knock landing on y or z read as hollow and a nullable-axis route reported readings — both now emit the disjunction `acceleration_carries_any_value` models, from axis names each specification owns, and the sensor reports reading events and value-bearing readings as two numbers. The `Try` arm gained its own byte-exact serialization fixtures, because a substring check proves an arm was reached and nothing about what it produced. **PARKED HERE, with the unfinished work carried as roadmap rows rather than prose (2026-08-07).** Two release receipts were executed before parking: the complete emitted client passes `node --check` — the whole assembled program, not the three isolated `Try` fixtures — and a playback run drove the emitted page and the emitted client together, crossing both sides of rest, ending at the model-supplied zero, restarting coherently on a second tap, staying still under reduced motion, and replacing both *not yet run* lines with exact observations. That playback ran in a DOM implementation, NOT a rendering engine: the build host had no way to install one, and calling the substitute a browser receipt would be the fabricated-plausible-output failure aimed at the one claim a reader cannot check. It is owed as part of `instrument-motion-physical-qualification`. A preview of the emitted bytes was published for a human ruling, and the probes run there returned `DeviceMotionEvent · 0 events, 0 carrying values, over 2002ms` and `LinearAccelerationSensor · construction refused · SecurityError` — which is the Permissions Policy path the lane opened on, observed live rather than reasoned about, and says NOTHING about the host's hardware; it is a fact about the harness. No feel verdict was given, so the tuning constants stay `AuditionProvisional` and no constants commit was made. The three named successors are `instrument-motion-physical-qualification` (executed browser receipts, the idle/typing negative control, calibration, and the rendering-engine playback), `instrument-rendered-control-coverage` (the false-green denominator that omits the theme, clear and activity controls), and `discarded-bool-result-wall` (census then refuse a Boolean computed in non-terminal position — the class two keystones in this lane were caught by). **Rung, honestly:** a support claim without a derived qualification is unreachable at the `TargetCapabilityVerdict` seam, scoped to that seam because the carriers stay publicly constructible and the guarantee is that no CONSUMER accepts a pre-judged one; hardware reachability on any real host is UNOBSERVED, the roster carries obligations rather than support claims, and `bob_impulse_requirement_candidate` stands at `AuditionProvisional`. **NOT closed**, stated because the first attempt claimed more than it had: an executed Chrome receipt, an executed Safari receipt, a qualified physical tap, a physical sensor reaching `PhysicalImpulse`, native macOS access, Windows/mobile/Linux bindings, the false-green dead-hover law, node-level syntax validation of the emitted client, and a DOM playback receipt — the client counts events and prints prose, so this is the synthetic bob plus RAW capability probes. The superseded `PhysicalImpulseSource`/`SensorAvailability` path in `gunbc.design.instrument_physical` carries its dissolution trigger rather than being deleted while the old page still renders it."),
      li(text: "**hierarchical design-register library — lift `gunbc.site` L1/L2 into `gunbc.design.*` (DOC-ONLY until #6927 merges).** PR #6927 proves the register on gunb.ai; the follow-on extracts site-independent L1 house walls + L2 role-material machinery (lift-not-fork), re-grounds contrast at L0 (WCAG/OKLCH, dissolving the HSL lightness-gap proxy in `theme_contrast_ok`), and stages L4 archetypes + `DataVizArea` behind second-consumer discipline. Phase A fires on #6927 merge with an emission-identical witness. The **palette catalogue** rode ahead additively (operator 2026-07-21): `extdeps.color.srgb` (CSS Color 4 cite; hex = a serialization of `Srgb8`, HSL→sRGB re-homed out of the WCAG module) → `gunbc.design.palette` (approval IS the computed WCAG grade — ordered figure-on-ground pairings total by construction) → `gunbc.design.catalog` (first entry island-pastel incl. the derived `loam` ink), witnessed by execution; its measured receipts (HSL-S misreads pastel tints as neon — ivory C 0.007 refused while `day_canvas` C 0.008 passes; gamer-cyan C 0.088 < mint C 0.142, so the envelope must be a per-area L×C region) are A.2's discriminating witness — see plan §2.2. → [design-register library](docs/plans/design-register-library.md)"),
      li(text: "**module identity vs storage — the path⇄module binding authority + law-governed bidirectional surfaces (operator-directed 2026-07-18).** A load-bearing file-path literal is an edge living in prose — invisible to affected-set, resolution, content-addressing, and effects. The priced incident has two axes: 03_normalize's behavioral receipt had **zero executing consumers** (orphaned enrollment, the #6775 regression's operative escape), while `sn_source_rel: String = \"src/v2/compiler/03_normalize.dag\"` hid the real dependency (safe-but-unscalable under today's never-skip default, a latent false-skip under precise selection). Model correction: the containment tree supplies a module's actual member relation and immediate/descendant closure; `GroupMembership.ByContainment` only classifies that membership's provenance on a roster — it is not a group algebra or module instance. A file is a storage realization; the many-to-many end state is staged against today's enforced 1:1 parse snapshot. `v2.compiler.source_authority` owns the derived path⇄module rows with honest provenance `ParsedFromSource | DeclarationScanned | ProducedByBehavior`; hand frontier rows and `build_module_path_index` both dissolve into projections. Code references modules; host boundaries project paths. Construction order: typed `SourceRef` makes bare-string file dependencies unwritable; an effect-reach census finds falsely declared hermetic reach, inventories migration, and dissolves after the boundary lands. Phase 0 requires **every witness row to name an executing consumer**; known-red quarantines execute expecting red, so greening is counted and enrolled-with-zero-executions itself reds. Per-surface authority is `(module × target × root) → (paths, authority, AuthoringSurface)`; Rust `SeedRetained` means the seed is authority and never BothWays, while `SelfEmitted` may opt into a declared lossless fragment. `BothWays` is formally a **quotient delta lens**, not just `ingest(emit(authority)) == authority`: unchanged-view stability, supported-edit re-projection, sequential coherence, source-only retention/declared canonicalization, located refusal without mutation, and versioned grammar/equivalence/bounds all execute. `DecodeFidelity.Lossless` proves endpoint decoding over a fragment; it does not reconstruct an authored operation that did not survive. md↔dag remains delta-first: files capture and project a graph delta keyed by stable containment address, never rendered position; the three-way file comparison is a named capture adapter that conservatively refuses intersecting keys until source-intent integration proves joint obligations. Overlap requests deeper reconciliation, disjoint keys may still contradict, and last-writer-wins never enters. Surface laws, behavioral receipt, and regen byte drift remain separate oracle families. → [module identity vs storage design](docs/plans/module-identity-storage-binding-design.md)"),
      li(text: "**gunbc-served dashboard + in-process dispatch (roadmap belt B, parent-scoped 2026-07-20).** The srv1 dashboard is an emitted-JS static server (`node_http_server_emit`) — a §3 dual representation of route-matching the substrate already models, re-frozen per deploy — and belt A's click path has that Node process subprocess-exec `gunbc run` per click (an env-var channel at a runtime-present site, the shell→intent routing class). B replaces both: `gunbc serve` (a thin seed accept-loop — parse/write only, the same altitude as `WitnessBin.Run`'s argv seam) calls ONE `.dag` entry `roadmap_serve_handle`; route selection is a pure fold over a `ServedRoute<H>` sum extending `ServedStaticRoute` (extdeps owns the shape, the handler vocabulary + invoke live with the gunbc instantiation — dispatch peripheral, §3), `POST /dispatch/\{node_id\}` invokes `belt_dispatch_node` in-process (the single dispatch authority, #6914 — per-variant status mapping, fail-closed), and `match_path_template` is the forward reading of the same `PathTemplate` the Node emitter reads backward into a regex (§4). Host paths (`/opt/gunbc/gunbc` tree · `/opt/gunbc/bin/gunbc` · `/opt/gunbc/dispatch-worktrees`) ground on ONE `gunbc.host_layout` authority consumed by the belt AND the live_deploy membership (members flip: ServeBinary + GunbcSourceTree + DispatchWorktreeRoot in, server.js out, nodejs Ensured-never-torn-down — R5). The interpreted accept-loop lands as a declared Scaffold, dissolution = emit-on-demand of the serve closure (ROADMAP §④); A's interim Node exec route + manual srv1 provisioning dissolve on B live. → [gunbc-served dashboard design](docs/plans/gunbc-served-dashboard-design.md)"),
      li(text: "**SCM economics — the GitLab 10-K corpus, modeled and witnessed (operator-directed 2026-07-25).** Economics and serving-cost grounding for the .dag-native SCM lane; the 2026-07-27 source-intent revision supersedes only the earlier node-grain integration premise: before building an SCM, model what running one costs and how one is priced, from primary sources only. All five GitLab 10-Ks (FY2022–FY2026, plus the FY2020/21 comparatives in the first — seven periods) live-pulled from EDGAR companyconcept and landed as cited rows: the full face income statement per year (cross-foots exactly in all seven), purchase-obligation/hosting commitments (instant-period facts), MD&A operating metrics (DBNRR as a typed lower-bound-vs-exact sum, ARR≥100k customer counts), and the live pricing model (Premium 29 USD listed seat-month, Ultimate de-listed to `CustomNegotiatedPricing`, usage meters incl. Duo credits). Mechanism landed with it: `FiledPeriod = FiledDuration | FiledInstant` (a half-specified citation period is unwritable, §5); the signed carrier `SignedUsdWholeDollars` (sign-as-sum, the GroupCompletion shape; dissolve-on `Int = GroupCompletion<Nat>` grounding) so filed losses stop being unrepresentable in the Nat-counted money magnitude; **citations required, not optional** (review 2026-07-25 — for a filed fact the citation IS the grounding, so an `Absent` arm was a fail-open invitation; zero uncited rows existed, so the field is plainly required with no legacy arm); and the **subject/source ruling** (operator 2026-07-25): the folder says which upstream AUTHORITY produced the facts, a typed edge says which ENTITY they are about — orthogonal axes, and a filename encodes neither. Every issuer facts module now carries a typed `IssuerCatalogRow` subject edge and `extdeps.pricing.gitlab_subscription` carries a `Vendor` subject edge, so filed financials and list prices correlate through the graph instead of a shared filename prefix (witnessed: the two GitLab fact families resolve to one entity; distinct issuers stay distinct). **The comparable set is three issuers, all SIC 7372:** GitLab (pure-play, seven periods), Atlassian (nearest pure-play comparable incl. Bitbucket — FY2022–FY2025, cross-foots exactly), Microsoft (GitHub's owner, the scale contrast — FY2022–FY2025, cross-foots exactly). The wider corpus exercises arms GitLab's seven loss-making years could not: `NonNegativeUsd` (Microsoft profitable throughout; Atlassian profitable FY2022 then three loss years) and `Unfiled` (Microsoft's FY2022 `OperatingExpenses`, untagged in its own 10-K — refused, never back-computed). It also proves the per-row element grain: Atlassian **switched cost elements mid-series** (`CostOfRevenue` FY2023/24 → `CostOfGoodsAndServicesSold` FY2025), each row carrying the tag its own filing used. Serving-cost ordering across the set, derived: GitLab 87.4% gross margin > Atlassian 82.8% > Microsoft 68.8% — the pure-play SCM is the cheapest-to-serve of the three. Typed gap: GitHub-level economics are NOT filed anywhere (Microsoft reports three segments, GitHub has no line of its own and segment facts are dimensional) — `microsoft_github_disaggregation_gap_note`, no estimate stored. Filed, not fixed: `Vendor<Domain>` parameterizes the entity rather than the relationship, so GitLab as the first two-domain entity (issuer + SaaS vendor) cannot inhabit both without forking its row — a ~14-file migration on the grounding-inventory lane with this PR as its discriminating receipt. The reads answering the operator's question are derived, never stored (§2): serving the product costs 11–13% of revenue (gross margin pinned in the high-80s all seven years); the real spend is selling (S&M share of revenue fell monotonically 122%→45.5% across all seven years) and R&D (~29%); GAAP operating income negative in all seven periods, narrowing −211M→−70M FY2023→FY2026; DBNRR decayed >152%→118% as the base matured; forward hosting commitments ≈ 1.1× annual cost of revenue. The launch-cost question (\"what would 10k free users cost to serve?\") is answered both ways in `gunbc.econ.free_tier_serving`: top-down, cost of revenue over the filed registered-user floor (`RegisteredUserCountRow`, \">50 million\" FY2026) upper-bounds average serving at ≤2.42 USD/user-year; bottom-up, a declared per-user envelope priced against the cited Hetzner rows floors 10k users at 2×AX41-1-LTD ≈ 115 EUR/month (knowable-wedge shape — refuse-on-unknown assumptions; egress allowance stays a declared assumption, dissolve-on citing Hetzner traffic terms into the row). Witnesses: `dag/test/claim/gitlab_10k_income_series_witness_test.dag` (24 claim families, 5 RED controls) · `dag/test/claim/free_tier_serving_witness_test.dag` (2 sized floors + 2 gleans, 7 RED controls) · `dag/test/claim/scm_issuer_corpus_witness_test.dag` (15 families over the Microsoft/Atlassian series, the entity edges, and 4 RED controls), green by execution. Residue, typed: revenue disaggregation (subscription/license, SaaS/self-managed) is dimension-tagged only — `gitlab_revenue_disaggregation_gap_note`, dissolve-on Financial Statement Data Sets ingestion; `fiscal_period` stays the inventoried bare string. **Serving-side vendor economics landed beside the issuer corpus** (operator question 2026-07-25, \"where are those cost tables, and how do we calculate compute/quota/network usage?\"): `extdeps.pricing.object_storage` carries cited object-storage rows — AWS S3 storage tiers, egress tiers, and request tiers from the machine-readable Price List API (the same authority `extdeps.pricing.aws_ec2` already cites), Google Cloud Storage standard + its separately-priced inter-region replication, Cloudflare R2 storage/ops/zero-egress — while Backblaze B2 and Hetzner Object Storage land as `RateUnavailable\{cause\}` rows because their headline rates are client-rendered and unreadable by this fetch (refused, never filled from memory). Two facts the rows make first-class: **durability is a cited vendor attribute**, not folklore (`Durability=99.999999999%` sits on the S3 price-list row itself — eleven nines, erasure-coded across facilities, which is *why* multiplying an object-storage rate by a replica count double-charges for redundancy already bought; replica multiplication belongs on self-managed block storage, and the two carriers say so to each other), and **the unit word is not the unit** (S3 quotes `GB-Mo`, GCS quotes `1 gibibyte hour`, none of the surfaces states whether G is 10⁹ or 2³⁰ — a 7.4% spread wider than the gap between several vendors, so every row carries its quoted label verbatim plus a typed `ByteBasis`, and cross-vendor comparison requires a *declared* assumption or refuses). `gunbc.econ.scm_serving_model` turns a workload profile into a bill on four axes — capacity held, network moved, operations counted, and compute *deliberately delegated* to the existing wedge/free-tier carriers rather than modeled twice — with a construction wall that makes the cheapest-looking wrong number unwritable: rows from different vendors refuse (`VendorMismatchAcrossRows`), so \"S3 storage with R2's free egress\" cannot be assembled. The derived read on one 10k-user profile (the same 100 MB stored / 500 MB egress envelope the free-tier floor declares): metered-egress S3 ≈ 474.80 USD/month of which **egress is 94.8%**, versus zero-egress R2 ≈ 16.62 USD/month — a ~28× spread on identical usage, which is why an SCM's serving bill is a *network* question, not a storage one. **Widened to a provider × magnitude matrix** (operator 2026-07-25): eight vendors now carry rows — AWS S3, Azure Blob, Google Cloud Storage, Cloudflare R2, Wasabi, Storj, Backblaze B2, Hetzner Object Storage — with **progressive tier application** (`progressive_cost`, a fold applying each band to its own slice like tax brackets), because at a million users this profile holds 100 TB and moves 500 TB/month, past AWS's 50 TB storage step and all three of its egress steps; flat-rate multiplication is only correct below the first boundary. Azure turns out to be the one vendor pricing **redundancy as an explicit purchasable ladder** (LRS → ZRS → GRS → GZRS → RA-GZRS, +25%/+120%/+181% on capacity at the first tier, and the same mechanism reappears on writes: a GZRS write is 2.35× an LRS write), published through an unauthenticated retail price API — so the operator's \"decide the reliability requirement first, then price what satisfies it\" is a typed filter: `ReliabilityRequirement` over (durability nines × redundancy scope) with a **three-valued** verdict where `RequirementUnknown` is neither pass nor fail (a vendor silent on geo-redundancy has not satisfied a geo-redundancy requirement — collapsing that to a pass is the absorbing fallback §5 forbids). Redundancy scope is admitted only where the vendor's own surface *names* it (Azure in its SKU, Storj in its distribution claim); everyone else is `RedundancyUnstated` and returns Unknown. The matrix, derived and witnessed at three magnitudes (USD/month): at 10k users S3 474.80 · Azure LRS 448.90 · Storj 42.00 · R2 16.62 · Wasabi 7.99; at 1M users S3 31,322 · Azure GZRS 33,677 · Azure LRS 30,993 · Storj 4,200 · R2 1,662 · Wasabi 799 — metered-egress vendors run ~19–39× the zero-egress ones on identical usage, and tiering makes AWS *sublinear* (6.9× cost for 10× users) while flat-rate vendors stay exactly linear (a witnessed contrast). **Stressed to agent-driven rates, and the ranking inverts** (operator 2026-07-25 — \"LLM programming is more stressful than humans writing code\"): the human-authored profile loads the wrong axis. Raising per-user object access four orders of magnitude (200 reads/20 writes → 2,000,000 logical *node* reads/200,000 node writes per user-month, roughly one agent working continuously) while raising the byte axes only single-digit multiples (dedup flattens what agents *store* far more than what they *touch*) moves operations from **0.4% of a request-metered bill to 99.5% of it**. At 10k users the agentic bill is R2 16,275 USD/month · S3 19,866 · Storj 175 · **Wasabi 39.95** — so the no-request-fee vendors win by **>400×**, and R2 goes from second-cheapest on the human profile to second-*most*-expensive on the agentic one (a witnessed inversion, both directions asserted). At 1M users unpacked it is 1.63M USD/month on R2 against 3,995 on Wasabi. The architectural consequence, modeled as `PackingPolicy` because it is the load-bearing choice rather than an implementation detail: **per-request billing punishes fine granularity**, so a node-grain store that maps one node to one object pays a fee per node — packing 1000 nodes per object (git's own packfile move) collapses the same R2 bill from 16,275 to 91.20 USD/month, a 178× reduction, with the byte axes provably untouched. Two design rules fall out: request pricing, not storage rate, is the selection criterion for an agentic SCM, and `UnpackedNodeGrain` — the shape a naive content-addressed store falls into by default — is the expensive default worth making visible in the type. Carriers: `dag/extdeps/pricing/object_storage.dag` · `dag/gunbc/econ/scm_serving_model.dag` · `dag/test/claim/scm_serving_model_witness_test.dag` (11 families, 6 RED controls incl. the basis, vendor-blend, and uncited-rate refusals) · `dag/test/claim/scm_provider_matrix_witness_test.dag` (13 families, incl. the tier-fold arithmetic, the flat-rate-overstates RED, and the four reliability-verdict controls) · `dag/test/claim/scm_agentic_stress_witness_test.dag` (11 families: the packing fold, the ops-share flip, the ranking inversion, and the seven-figure unpacked case); `std.measure` gained the three storage billing-unit markers on the existing `MoneyRate<P>` axis. Carriers: `dag/extdeps/sec/\{facts,us_gaap,operating_metrics\}.dag` · `dag/extdeps/sec/issuer/\{gitlab,microsoft,atlassian\}\{,_facts\}.dag` · `dag/extdeps/vendor/\{microsoft,atlassian\}.dag` · `dag/extdeps/pricing/gitlab_subscription.dag` · `dag/gunbc/econ/free_tier_serving.dag`. **Regional compute, measured across every geography** (operator question 2026-07-25, \"wages in India are much lower — is compute cheaper there?\"): no, and not anywhere sampled. `extdeps.pricing.regional_compute` holds one SKU fixed per provider across 22 cited rows — AWS `m7i.large` over 10 regions from the Price List API, Azure `Standard_D2s_v5` over 12 from the retail API — so a price gap is a REGION fact, not a hardware fact. Every region prices above its provider's US baseline: Mumbai +5.2%, Frankfurt +19.8%, UAE +22.6%, Singapore +25%, Tokyo +29.2%, Cape Town +32.3%, Hong Kong +37.5%, São Paulo **+59.4%** — and the two vendors agree to the basis point on Brazil (5937 bp on both, independently derived). Datacenter cost is globally-priced capital, power, cooling and transit; wages are a thin slice of hyperscale opex, so a low-wage economy does not buy cheap compute and the arbitrage runs the other way (buy where cheap, serve outward — an SCM's clone path is bandwidth-bound, not round-trip-bound). Two structural findings landed as rows rather than prose: instance families are NOT uniformly available (Bahrain has no `m7i`, so its row carries `m6i.large` and says the comparison is therefore not like-for-like), and **local/regional providers essentially never publish machine-readable prices** — E2E Networks (India), Magalu (Brazil), Afrihost (South Africa), Alibaba and Contabo all land as `HourlyUnavailable\{cause\}`, while Sakura Internet (Japan) is readable but gets the distinct third arm `HourlyUnrepresentable` because it quotes JPY and `std.currency` admits only Eur/Usd (dissolve-on: adding Jpy, a legitimate std addition unlike a vendor billing unit). `gunbc.econ.regional_compute_premium` derives the basis-point premium and refuses across providers (`ProviderMismatch`) and vCPU counts, so an AWS-vs-Azure number cannot masquerade as a region comparison. Witness: `dag/test/claim/regional_compute_witness_test.dag` (10 families, 4 RED controls), green by execution. Roadmap lane: §2 \"SCM — source-intent integration, visibility-first\" (operator-directed 2026-07-25; premise revised 2026-07-27) → [dag-scm design](docs/plans/dag-scm-design.md)."),
      li(text: "**source-intent integration — SCM-compatible, not SCM-shaped (operator-directed 2026-07-27).** Supersedes only the earlier node-identity/keyed-diff integration premise; the economics and visibility-first order remain. Product contract FIRST: the user makes changes and states preferences; the system owns integration. The native flow is **edit → submit → Landed**. At the semantic boundary the only semantic handoffs are **Landed** or **Choice required**; the latter asks one genuine domain-language preference with concrete result previews and a stored continuation, and one answer resumes automatically. The overall primary outcome adds **Could not land** for a terminal non-semantic refusal after every permitted retry, observation, repair, approval route, or other machine-owned continuation is exhausted. It gives a plain domain reason, responsible owner/real next action, confirms no transition committed, and retains the typed/located/counted refusal for inspect; permanent authorization/policy/bound/effect failures never disappear into inspect-only state or masquerade as preferences. Every machine-answerable issue stays inside the machine while a continuation remains. No normal journey requires merge, rebase, cherry-pick, reset, conflict-marker editing, or force-push, and primary messages exclude proof/evidence/solver vocabulary; full receipts and mechanics remain inspectable on demand. Internally, a recorded fact becomes evidence only through a scoped claim relation carrying polarity, inference rule, authority/provenance, freshness, fidelity, probe independence, and maximum conclusion. That missing carrier lands separately at P−1 with Codex provider readiness, `os_install_deduction`, and SCM as discriminating consumers; `std.realization_reconcile.Grounding`, `readback_independence`, and `ObservationVerdict` are partial precedents, not the relation. `GroupMembership.ByContainment` classifies membership provenance only; the containment authority supplies a module's actual member relation/closure. Source intent means an authenticated authored transformation plus explicit reads/relies, modifies/frame, ensures/guarantees, dependencies, atomicity, occurrence identity, and authority — never a mental preference inferred from endpoints. ONE public reconciliation operation composes domain facts/rules/checkable certificates, never pairwise feature cases; candidate generation is a bounded finite closure, because today's `find_witness` consumes caller-supplied candidates and `solve_constraints` closes only a singleton scaffold. The receipt separates claim assessment (support/challenge/both/neither), semantic closed-zero/one/many/unclosed, admission (including unauthorized/approval/policy refusal), transition (`Committed | RetryStaleParent | EffectFailed`), and projection; **Applied means committed + independent read-back**, not merely one candidate. The primary projection is `Landed | ChoiceRequired | CouldNotLand`: unclosed machine work, stale retry, authorization routing, effect retry, and projection repair never masquerade as questions, but a terminal refusal is always visible. Proposal occurrence dedup is idempotent; arbitrary transformations are not. Parallel patches require witnessed context transport and a commuting square/joinability modulo declared equivalence; batch partition and n-way obligations hold, and the accepted parent policy/equivalence/claim set cannot be self-amended by the proposal under evaluation. Safety/authorization/confidentiality/exact-parent are hard constraints; then maximize safe partial information/work, then minimize judgment/CI/compute under Pareto order. Timestamps and arrival never establish semantic precedence. Git, Mercurial, and Pijul land as independent `extdeps/` upstream models before DFS extracts any shared realization roles; the core has no vendor enum or switch, and adding a fourth SCM cannot edit the kernel or primary interaction. P3 consumes a bound target-transition capability; Git `update-ref <ref> <new> <old>` is the first writable realization, not the interface. Git remains the first ordinary worktree/import/export surface, with exact endpoint delta distinct from native authored operation and modes/tags/symlinks/gitlinks/attributes/object-format/unavailable-object fidelity preserved; other upstreams retain their own identity, history, target, and publication facts or refuse capability honestly. → [source-intent integration design](docs/plans/dag-scm-design.md)"),
    ]),
  ]
}

fn building_checks_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "Building & checks"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "`cargo test --workspace` · `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` · `cargo fmt --all --check`"),
      li(text: "one-time per clone: `git config core.hooksPath .githooks` — the only documented manual seed; generated pre-commit/pre-push hooks then idempotently converge `merge.generated-artifact.driver` and re-assert `core.hooksPath` via argv derived from `gunbc.repo_local_git_config` (clones that skip hooksPath degrade to vanilla text-merge for generated-artifact paths; drift gate still guards at CI). The driver REFUSES rather than answering `true`: git reaches a low-level merge driver only when both sides changed the path since the merge base — measured on a four-case matrix, one-sided and identical changes never reach it — and taking the ours side there dropped the other side's authority-derived bytes with no conflict, twice on #7836 against the stage0 seed. It now leaves the ours side in the worktree with no conflict markers, marks the path unmerged, and prints the regeneration recipe; the class is mechanically preventable, not structural, and its next-rung trigger is the commit-writer binding rows in `gunbc.commit_workflow`"),
      li(text: "explicit actuator (CI / tooling): `gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry dag/gunbc/repo_local_git_config.dag --function converge`"),
      li(text: "CI — **RUNG DROP, DECLARED (2026-08-15, the floor cut).** WHAT WAS HERE: one composed floor pass, `claim_executor` with a `--plan-entry` naming `src/v2/workflow/ci_floor_plan.dag`, a v2 scheduler deciding batches from `gunbc.ci_spec`, a compile-clean gate ordered ahead of everything else, per-PR discovery over `CiSpec.discovery_scan_dirs`, and a 4-hourly `affected-set-falsifier` cadence carrying the whole-tree cold controls. **ALL OF IT IS DELETED** — the workflows (`ci.yml`, `falsifier.yml`, `falsifier-alert.yml`), their `.dag` authorities, and ~30 witness modules. This paragraph previously recited that invocation in the present tense; it was false the moment the cut landed, and a knowingly-false recital in the canonical authority is premise contamination — every session reading it plans against a command that does not exist. WHAT RUNS NOW: one emission, `gunbc.witness_floor_workflow` → `.github/workflows/witnesses.yml`, invoking our own binary once — `claim_executor --required-floor --source-root dag --source-root src/v2` — which folds every discovered witness through one prepared subject via `v2.workflow.required_floor` `run_required_floor`. No plan entry, no plan function, no batch id, no worker role, no selection flag: the fold has no such concepts to configure — which PRESERVES the 2026-08-13 operator directive that the whole discovered roster runs unshrunk, by construction rather than by a flag someone must remember to set. That directive's own carrier (`corpus_selection_off_note`) died with the floor; `gunbc.ci_spec` retains the history and now points here. **THE FOLD NOW EXECUTES, measured 2026-08-19.** Three consecutive green runs on main, the latest `32203661157`: `planned=9008 executed=9008 terminal=9008 passed=8702 known_red_held=306 failed=0 stale_quarantine=0 budget_refused=0`, ~30 min wall. This clause previously read that no witness had executed and that the fold refused during preparation; that was true when written and is now false, and it is corrected in place rather than left standing until the rest of the paragraph can be rewritten — because a knowingly-false recital is premise contamination whichever direction it points, and this one had already cost real work: a session measuring the TestClaim orphan population read it, concluded the floor had never run, and raised a sequencing question about roster growth that does not exist. The rung drop below is unaffected: the fold running green establishes that the replacement executes, not that the re-add queue has closed. WHAT IS UNGUARDED IN THE MEANTIME, named rather than left to be rediscovered: the generated-artifact drift gates, heal, the seven effect gates, the fmt gate, merge-admission stamping, the falsifier cadence, and the per-witness eval deadline that `gunbc_ci_fast_lane_witness_eval_budget` used to arm (`gunbc.witness_row_cost` `gunbc_ci_fast_lane_rule_note` carries that one's own drop). Each is a separate re-add, each re-derived from its own first principles under its own operator agreement rather than restored from the deleted machinery. RESTORATION TRIGGER: this paragraph is rewritten as an ordinary present-tense description — not amended, rewritten in one pass — when the re-add queue closes. Until then it describes a rung drop, and describing the replacement as finished would be the inflation §4b names as worse than sitting low. **WHAT SURVIVES UNTOUCHED, enumerated rather than swept away with the bullet** — this paragraph replaced a longer one, and a prose row is deleted for one reason and takes everything in it unless its contents are enumerated first (the same rule that governs deleting a witness file whole): the compile-clean scope authority `tools.dag_compile_clean_scope` and its import-closure selection (`entry_file_touched_via_import_closure`) are live and unmodified by the cut — what is gone is the CI *job* that invoked them, not the authority; the `regen_input_sources` import closure survives and is now read by `v1_compiler.required_regen_host` — but the `regen_stage0` binary that consumed it, and the `RegenVerifyGate` / `SelfHostStalenessGate` pair that invoked it, are DELETED at the root (the regen cut), so the self-host fixed point is answered by `claim_executor --required-regen-fixed-point` and by nothing else. This clause previously read that the fixed point and its closure both survived with only their job removed; that was true when written and the regen cut falsified its first half, so it is rewritten here rather than annotated — a second sentence beside it would be two accounts of one fact; and parse remains grammar-owned — `ingest_yaml_source` (`dag/extdeps/languages/yaml/ingest.dag`), no shell or host parser — which was never a floor fact at all and is restated here so that dropping the bullet does not drop it. **TWO OPERATOR RULINGS ALSO LIVED IN THE REPLACED BULLET AND STILL HOLD**, restated because a ruling about what CI does *not* do is invisible once the paragraph describing CI is rewritten, and nothing in the new mechanism would contradict it loudly: the Rust test suite was removed from CI 2026-07-11 (operator ruling, recorded at `gunbc.commit_workflow` `commit_gate_rust_suite_removed_disposition`) and runs locally only; and clippy was removed from CI 2026-07-08, because a crate-wide `#![allow(clippy::all)]` made it zero-signal over ~44 minutes per run, leaving it a local dev check. Neither is reinstated by the replacement, and neither is the floor cut's to reverse."),
    ]),
  ]
}

fn design_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  flat_map([
    preamble_blocks(),
    section_1_blocks(),
    section_2_blocks(),
    section_3_blocks(),
    section_4_blocks(),
    section_4b_blocks(),
    section_4c_blocks(),
    section_5_blocks(),
    section_6_blocks(),
    section_7_blocks(),
    failure_modes_blocks(),
    open_threads_blocks(),
    building_checks_blocks(),
  ], bs => bs)
}

fn design_document() -> MarkdownDocument {
  MarkdownDocument { blocks: design_blocks() }
}

fn expected_design_md() -> String {
  serialize_markdown_source(doc: design_document())
}

fn design_drifted(committed: String) -> Bool {
  !(committed == expected_design_md())
}
